INSERT INTO `bills` VALUES
(73286,'hb267',30,'house','Assault or assault and battery;  affirmative defense, penalty.',98,'<b>Assault and battery; affirmative defense; penalty.</b> Provides an affirmative defense to prosecution of an individual for assault or assault and battery of certain specified individuals for which the enhanced Class 6 felony and six month mandatory minimum apply if such individual proves, by a preponderance of the evidence, that at the time of the assault or assault and battery (i) the individual\'s behaviors were a result of (a) mental illness or (b) a neurocognitive disorder, including dementia, or a neurodevelopmental disability, including a developmental disability or intellectual disability, such as autism spectrum disorder, as defined in the most recent edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders of the American Psychiatric Association or (ii) the individual met the criteria for issuance of an emergency custody order.  The bill provides that if such individual does not prove that his behaviors were a result of his mental illness, intellectual disability, developmental disability, or neurocognitive disorder but the evidence establishes that his mental illness, intellectual disability, developmental disability, or neurocognitive disorder otherwise contributed to his behaviors, the finder of fact may find the accused guilty of a misdemeanor assault or assault and battery. The bill also provides that such affirmative defense shall not be construed to allow an affirmative defense for voluntary intoxication. This bill is identical to SB 357.','<br><center><b>VIRGINIA ACTS OF ASSEMBLY -- CHAPTER</b></center>\r \r <i>An Act to amend and reenact § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-57\">18.2-57</a> of the Code of Virginia,\r relating to assault and battery; affirmative defense; penalty.</i>\r \r <center>[H 267]</center>\r \r <center>Approved</center>\r \r <p> </p>\r \r <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r \r <p>1. That § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-57\">18.2-57</a> of the Code of Virginia is amended and\r reenacted as follows:</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-57\">18.2-57</a>. Assault and battery; penalty.</p>\r \r <p>A. Any person who commits a simple assault or assault and\r battery is guilty of a Class 1 misdemeanor, and if the person intentionally\r selects the person against whom a simple assault is committed because of his\r race, religious conviction, gender, disability, gender identity, sexual\r orientation, color, or national origin, the penalty upon conviction shall\r include a term of confinement of at least six months.</p>\r \r <p>B. However, if a person intentionally selects the person\r against whom an assault and battery resulting in bodily injury is committed\r because of his race, religious conviction, gender, disability, gender identity,\r sexual orientation, color, or national origin, the person is guilty of a Class\r 6 felony, and the penalty upon conviction shall include a term of confinement\r of at least six months.</p>\r \r <p>C. In addition, if any person commits an assault or an assault\r and battery against another knowing or having reason to know that such other\r person is a judge, a magistrate, a law-enforcement officer as defined in\r subsection G, a correctional officer as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/53.1-1\">53.1-1</a>, a person directly\r involved in the care, treatment, or supervision of inmates in the custody of\r the Department of Corrections or an employee of a local or regional\r correctional facility directly involved in the care, treatment, or supervision\r of inmates in the custody of the facility, a person directly involved in the\r care, treatment, or supervision of persons in the custody of or under the\r supervision of the Department of Juvenile Justice, an employee or other\r individual who provides control, care, or treatment of sexually violent\r predators committed to the custody of the Department of Behavioral Health and\r Developmental Services, a firefighter as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/65.2-102\">65.2-102</a>, or a volunteer\r firefighter or any emergency medical services personnel member who is employed\r by or is a volunteer of an emergency medical services agency or as a member of\r a bona fide volunteer fire department or volunteer emergency medical services\r agency, regardless of whether a resolution has been adopted by the governing\r body of a political subdivision recognizing such firefighters or emergency\r medical services personnel as employees, engaged in the performance of his\r public duties anywhere in the Commonwealth, such person is guilty of a Class 6\r felony, and, upon conviction, the sentence of such person shall include a\r mandatory minimum term of confinement of six months.</p>\r \r <p><ins>It is an affirmative defense to prosecution of an\r individual for assault or assault and battery under this subsection if such\r individual proves, by a preponderance of the evidence, that at the time of the\r assault or assault and battery (i) the individual\'s behaviors were a result of\r (a) mental illness as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/37.2-100\">37.2-100</a> or (b) a neurocognitive disorder,\r including dementia, or an intellectual disability or a developmental disability\r such as autism spectrum disorder, as defined in the most recent edition of the\r Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders of the American\r Psychiatric Association, or (ii) the individual met the criteria for issuance\r of an emergency custody order pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/37.2-808\">37.2-808</a>.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>If such individual does not prove by a preponderance of the\r evidence that at the time of the assault or assault and battery his behaviors\r were a result of his mental illness, intellectual disability, developmental\r disability, or neurocognitive disorder but the evidence establishes that his\r mental illness, intellectual disability, developmental disability, or\r neurocognitive disorder otherwise contributed to his behaviors, the finder of\r fact may find the accused guilty of a misdemeanor pursuant to subsection A.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>Nothing in this subsection shall be construed to allow an\r affirmative defense for voluntary intoxication.</ins></p>\r \r <p>Nothing in this subsection shall be construed to affect the\r right of any person charged with a violation of this section from asserting and\r presenting evidence in support of any defenses to the charge that may be\r available under common law.</p>\r \r <p>D. In addition, if any person commits a battery against\r another knowing or having reason to know that such other person is a full-time\r or part-time employee of any public or private elementary or secondary school\r and is engaged in the performance of his duties as such, he is guilty of a\r Class 1 misdemeanor and the sentence of such person upon conviction shall\r include a sentence of 15 days in jail, two days of which shall be a mandatory\r minimum term of confinement. However, if the offense is committed by use of a\r firearm or other weapon prohibited on school property pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-308.1\">18.2-308.1</a>,\r the person shall serve a mandatory minimum sentence of confinement of six\r months.</p>\r \r <p>E. In addition, any person who commits a battery against\r another knowing or having reason to know that such individual is a health care\r provider as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/8.01-581.1\">8.01-581.1</a> who is engaged in the performance of his\r duties in a hospital or in an emergency room on the premises of any clinic or\r other facility rendering emergency medical care is guilty of a Class 1\r misdemeanor. The sentence of such person, upon conviction, shall include a term\r of confinement of 15 days in jail, two days of which shall be a mandatory\r minimum term of confinement.</p>\r \r <p>F. In addition, any person who commits an assault or an\r assault and battery against another knowing or having reason to know that such\r individual is an operator of a vehicle operated by a public transportation\r service as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-160.2\">18.2-160.2</a> who is engaged in the performance of his\r duties is guilty of a Class 1 misdemeanor. The sentence of such person, upon\r conviction, shall also prohibit such person from entering or riding in any\r vehicle operated by the public transportation service that employed such operator\r for a period of not less than six months as a term and condition of such\r sentence.</p>\r \r <p>G. As used in this section:</p>\r \r <p>&quot;Disability&quot; means a physical or mental impairment\r that substantially limits one or more of a person\'s major life activities.</p>\r \r <p>&quot;Hospital&quot; means a public or private institution\r licensed pursuant to Chapter 5 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/32.1-123\">32.1-123</a> et seq.) of Title 32.1 or Article 2\r (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/37.2-403\">37.2-403</a> et seq.) of Chapter 4 of Title 37.2.</p>\r \r <p>&quot;Judge&quot; means any justice or judge of a court of\r record of the Commonwealth including a judge designated under § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/17.1-105\">17.1-105</a>, a\r judge under temporary recall under § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/17.1-106\">17.1-106</a>, or a judge pro tempore under §\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/17.1-109\">17.1-109</a>, any member of the State Corporation Commission, or of the Virginia\r Workers\' Compensation Commission, and any judge of a district court of the\r Commonwealth or any substitute judge of such district court.</p>\r \r <p>&quot;Law-enforcement officer&quot; means any full-time or\r part-time employee of a police department or sheriff\'s office that is part of\r or administered by the Commonwealth or any political subdivision thereof who is\r responsible for the prevention or detection of crime and the enforcement of the\r penal, traffic or highway laws of the Commonwealth, any conservation officer of\r the Department of Conservation and Recreation commissioned pursuant to §\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-115\">10.1-115</a>, any special agent of the Virginia Alcoholic Beverage Control Authority,\r conservation police officers appointed pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/29.1-200\">29.1-200</a>, full-time sworn\r members of the enforcement division of the Department of Motor Vehicles\r appointed pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/46.2-217\">46.2-217</a>, and any employee with internal investigations\r authority designated by the Department of Corrections pursuant to subdivision\r 11 of § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/53.1-10\">53.1-10</a>, and such officer also includes jail officers in local and\r regional correctional facilities, all deputy sheriffs, whether assigned to\r law-enforcement duties, court services or local jail responsibilities,\r auxiliary police officers appointed or provided for pursuant to §§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/15.2-1731\">15.2-1731</a>\r and <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/15.2-1733\">15.2-1733</a>, auxiliary deputy sheriffs appointed pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/15.2-1603\">15.2-1603</a>,\r police officers of the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority pursuant to §\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/5.1-158\">5.1-158</a>, and fire marshals appointed pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/27-30\">27-30</a> when such fire\r marshals have police powers as set out in §§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/27-34.2\">27-34.2</a> and <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/27-34.2:1\">27-34.2:1</a>.</p>\r \r <p>&quot;School security officer&quot; means the same as that\r term is defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/9.1-101\">9.1-101</a>.</p>\r \r <p>H. &quot;Simple assault&quot; or &quot;assault and\r battery&quot; shall not be construed to include the use of, by any school\r security officer or full-time or part-time employee of any public or private\r elementary or secondary school while acting in the course and scope of his\r official capacity, any of the following: (i) incidental, minor or reasonable\r physical contact or other actions designed to maintain order and control; (ii)\r reasonable and necessary force to quell a disturbance or remove a student from\r the scene of a disturbance that threatens physical injury to persons or damage\r to property; (iii) reasonable and necessary force to prevent a student from\r inflicting physical harm on himself; (iv) reasonable and necessary force for\r self-defense or the defense of others; or (v) reasonable and necessary force to\r obtain possession of weapons or other dangerous objects or controlled\r substances or associated paraphernalia that are upon the person of the student\r or within his control.</p>\r \r <p>In determining whether a person was acting within the\r exceptions provided in this subsection, due deference shall be given to\r reasonable judgments that were made by a school security officer or full-time\r or part-time employee of any public or private elementary or secondary school\r at the time of the event.</p>','FH2122',5,'house','vetoed by governor','failed',NULL,'<p>The Fiscal Impact Statement indicates that the proposed bill, HB267, may have the effect of fewer people being sentenced to jail or prison. However, at this time, there is no available data to estimate the impact on jail or prison bed space needs. The fiscal impact on the Department of Health and Developmental Services and state and local law enforcement agencies is indeterminate and will be updated if additional information is received. Therefore, the specific cost of the legislation is not stated in the statement.</p><p>Summary generated automatically by OpenAI.</p>','878395a66e77ab97314ab7e0ec7c6a9a',125,0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-05','2024-11-17 00:55:04','2024-01-05 18:55:02');
