INSERT INTO `bills` VALUES
(74045,'sb332',30,'senate','Misdemeanor; maximum term of confinement.',532,'<b>Misdemeanor; maximum term of confinement.</b> Reduces from 12 months to 364 days the maximum term of confinement in jail for a Class 1 misdemeanor. The bill contains technical amendments. The bill also requires the Virginia Criminal Sentencing Commission to revise all labels on Sentencing Guidelines worksheets and instructions in the Sentencing Guidelines manual so that any conviction for an offense defined as a Class 1 misdemeanor or any other misdemeanor with a maximum penalty of 364 days of incarceration, as provided by the bill, shall be scored as if the maximum penalty were 12 months for the purposes of preparing and using the discretionary sentencing guidelines.','<br><center><b>SENATE BILL NO. 332 </b></center>\r \r <center>Senate Amendments in [ ] – January 17, 2024</center>\r \r <center>Prefiled January 9, 2024 </center>\r \r <i>A BILL to amend and reenact §§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-11\">18.2-11</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-279.6\">22.1-279.6</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/33.2-802\">33.2-802</a>,\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/36-85.12\">36-85.12</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.32\">62.1-44.32</a>, as it is currently effective and as it shall become effective,\r and <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:20\">62.1-44.34:20</a> of the Code of Virginia, relating to misdemeanor; maximum\r term of confinement.</i>\r \r <center>----------</center>\r \r <center>Patrons Prior to Engrossment--Senators Salim and Hashmi </center>\r \r <center>----------</center>\r \r <center>Referred to Committee for Courts of Justice </center>\r \r <center>----------</center>\r \r <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r \r <p>1. That §§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-11\">18.2-11</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-279.6\">22.1-279.6</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/33.2-802\">33.2-802</a>, <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/36-85.12\">36-85.12</a>,\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.32\">62.1-44.32</a>, as it is currently effective and as it shall become effective, and\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:20\">62.1-44.34:20</a> of the Code of Virginia are amended and reenacted as follows:</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-11\">18.2-11</a>. Punishment for conviction of misdemeanor.</p>\r \r <p>The authorized punishments for conviction of a misdemeanor\r are:</p>\r \r <p><s>(a) </s><ins>1. </ins>For Class 1 misdemeanors, confinement in\r jail for not more than<s> twelve months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a fine of not\r more than $2,500, either or both.</p>\r \r <p><s>(b) </s><ins>2. </ins>For Class 2 misdemeanors, confinement in\r jail for not more than six months and a fine of not more than $1,000, either or\r both.</p>\r \r <p><s>(c) </s><ins>3. </ins>For Class 3 misdemeanors, a fine of not\r more than $500.</p>\r \r <p><s>(d) </s><ins>4. </ins>For Class 4 misdemeanors, a fine of not\r more than $250.</p>\r \r <p>For a misdemeanor offense prohibiting proximity to children as\r described in subsection A of § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-370.2\">18.2-370.2</a>, the sentencing court is authorized\r to impose the punishment set forth in subsection B of that section in addition\r to any other penalty provided by law.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-279.6\">22.1-279.6</a>. Board of Education guidelines and model policies\r for codes of student conduct; school board regulations.</p>\r \r <p>A. The Board of Education shall establish guidelines and\r develop model policies for codes of student conduct to aid local school boards\r in the implementation of such policies. The guidelines and model policies shall\r include (i) criteria for the removal of a student from a class, the use of\r suspension, expulsion, and exclusion as disciplinary measures, the grounds for\r suspension and expulsion and exclusion, and the procedures to be followed in\r such cases, including proceedings for such suspension, expulsion, and exclusion\r decisions and all applicable appeals processes; (ii) standards, consistent with\r state, federal and case laws, for school board policies on alcohol and drugs,\r gang-related activity, hazing, vandalism, trespassing, threats, search and\r seizure, disciplining of students with disabilities, intentional injury of\r others, self-defense, bullying, the use of electronic means for purposes of\r bullying, harassment, and intimidation, and dissemination of such policies to\r students, their parents, and school personnel; (iii) standards for in-service\r training of school personnel in and examples of the appropriate management of\r student conduct and student offenses in violation of school board policies;\r (iv) standards for dress or grooming codes; and (v) standards for reducing bias\r and harassment in the enforcement of any code of student conduct.</p>\r \r <p>In accordance with the most recent enunciation of\r constitutional principles by the Supreme Court of the United States of America,\r the Board\'s standards for school board policies on alcohol and drugs and search\r and seizure shall include guidance for procedures relating to voluntary and\r mandatory drug testing in schools, including which groups may be tested, use of\r test results, confidentiality of test information, privacy considerations,\r consent to the testing, need to know, and release of the test results to the\r appropriate school authority.</p>\r \r <p>In the case of suspension and expulsion, the procedures set\r forth in this article shall be the minimum procedures that the school board may\r prescribe.</p>\r \r <p>B. School boards shall adopt and revise, as required by §\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-253.13:7\">22.1-253.13:7</a> and in accordance with the requirements of this section,\r regulations on codes of student conduct that are consistent with, but may be\r more stringent than, the guidelines of the Board. School boards shall include\r in the regulations on codes of student conduct procedures for suspension,\r expulsion, and exclusion decisions and shall biennially review the model\r student conduct code to incorporate discipline options and alternatives to\r preserve a safe, nondisruptive environment for effective teaching and learning.</p>\r \r <p>C. Each school board shall include in its code of student\r conduct prohibitions against hazing and profane or obscene language or conduct.\r School boards shall also cite in their codes of student conduct the provisions\r of § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-56\">18.2-56</a>, which defines and prohibits hazing and imposes a Class 1\r misdemeanor penalty for violations, that is, confinement in jail for not more\r than<s> 12 months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a fine of not more than $2,500,\r either or both.</p>\r \r <p>D. Each school board shall include in its code of student\r conduct policies and procedures that include a prohibition against bullying.\r Such policies and procedures shall (i) be consistent with the standards for\r school board policies on bullying and the use of electronic means for purposes\r of bullying developed by the Board pursuant to subsection A and (ii) direct the\r principal or his designee to notify the parent of any student involved in an\r alleged incident of bullying within 24 hours of learning of the allegation of\r bullying.</p>\r \r <p>Such policies and procedures shall not be interpreted to\r infringe upon the First Amendment rights of students and are not intended to\r prohibit expression of religious, philosophical, or political views, provided\r that such expression does not cause an actual, material disruption of the work\r of the school.</p>\r \r <p>E. A school board may regulate the use or possession of beepers\r or other portable communications devices and laser pointers by students on\r school property or attending school functions or activities and establish\r disciplinary procedures pursuant to this article to which students violating\r such regulations will be subject.</p>\r \r <p>F. Nothing in this section shall be construed to require any\r school board to adopt policies requiring or encouraging any drug testing in\r schools. However, a school board may, in its discretion, require or encourage\r drug testing in accordance with the Board of Education\'s guidelines and model\r student conduct policies required by subsection A and the Board\'s guidelines\r for student searches required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-279.7\">22.1-279.7</a>.</p>\r \r <p>G. The Board of Education shall establish standards to ensure\r compliance with the federal Improving America\'s Schools Act of 1994 (Part\r F-Gun-Free Schools Act of 1994), as amended, in accordance with § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-277.07\">22.1-277.07</a>.</p>\r \r <p>This subsection shall not be construed to diminish the\r authority of the Board of Education or to diminish the Governor\'s authority to\r coordinate and provide policy direction on official communications between the\r Commonwealth and the United States government.</p>\r \r <p>H. Each school board shall include in its code of student\r conduct a prohibition on possessing any tobacco product or nicotine vapor\r product, as those terms are defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/18.2-371.2\">18.2-371.2</a>, on a school bus, on school\r property, or at an on-site or off-site school-sponsored activity.</p>\r \r <p>I. Any school board may include in its code of student conduct\r a dress or grooming code. Any dress or grooming code included in a school\r board\'s code of student conduct or otherwise adopted by a school board shall\r (i) permit any student to wear any religiously and ethnically specific or\r significant head covering or hairstyle, including hijabs, yarmulkes, headwraps,\r braids, locs, and cornrows; (ii) maintain gender neutrality by subjecting any\r student to the same set of rules and standards regardless of gender; (iii) not\r have a disparate impact on students of a particular gender; (iv) be clear,\r specific, and objective in defining terms, if used; (v) prohibit any school\r board employee from enforcing the dress or grooming code by direct physical\r contact with a student or a student\'s attire; and (vi) prohibit any school\r board employee from requiring a student to undress in front of any other\r individual, including the enforcing school board employee, to comply with the\r dress or grooming code.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/33.2-802\">33.2-802</a>. Dumping trash; penalty.</p>\r \r <p>A. It is unlawful for any person to dump or otherwise dispose\r of trash, garbage, refuse, litter, a companion animal as defined in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/3.2-6500\">3.2-6500</a>\r for the purpose of disposal, or other unsightly matter on (i) public property,\r including a public highway, right-of-way, or property adjacent to such highway\r or right-of-way, or (ii) private property without the written consent of the\r owner or his agent.</p>\r \r <p>B. If a person is arrested for a violation of this section and\r the matter alleged to have been illegally dumped or disposed of has been\r ejected from a motor vehicle or transported to the disposal site in a motor\r vehicle, the arresting officer may comply with the provisions of § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/46.2-936\">46.2-936</a> in\r making an arrest.</p>\r \r <p>If a violation of this section has been observed by any person\r and the matter illegally dumped or disposed of has been ejected or removed from\r a motor vehicle, the owner or operator of the motor vehicle shall be presumed\r to be the person ejecting or disposing of the matter. However, such presumption\r shall be rebuttable by competent evidence.</p>\r \r <p>C. Any person convicted of a violation of this section is guilty\r of a misdemeanor punishable by confinement in jail for not more than<s> 12\r months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a fine of not less than $500 or more than\r $2,500, either or both. In lieu of the imposition of confinement in jail, the\r court may order the defendant to perform a mandatory minimum of 10 hours of\r community service in litter abatement activities.</p>\r \r <p>D. The governing body of any locality may adopt ordinances not\r in conflict with the provisions of this section and may repeal or amend such\r ordinances.</p>\r \r <p>E. The provisions of this section shall not apply to the\r lawful disposal of such matter in landfills.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/36-85.12\">36-85.12</a>. Violation; civil and criminal penalties.</p>\r \r <p>It<s> shall be</s><ins> is</ins> unlawful for any person, firm<ins>,</ins>\r or corporation<s>,</s> to violate any provisions of this law, the rules and regulations\r enacted under authority of this law, or the Federal<s> Law</s><ins> Act</ins> and<ins>\r Federal</ins> Regulations. Any person, firm<ins>,</ins> or corporation violating any\r provision of<s> said laws, rules and</s><ins> such law or</ins> regulations, or any\r final order issued thereunder, shall be liable for<ins> a</ins> civil penalty not\r to exceed $1,000 for each violation. Each violation shall constitute a separate\r violation with respect to each manufactured home or with respect to each\r failure or refusal to allow or to perform an act required by<s> the legislation</s><ins>\r such law</ins> or regulations. The maximum civil penalty may not exceed<s> one</s><ins>\r $1</ins> million<s> dollars</s> for any related series of violations occurring\r within one year from the date of the first violation.</p>\r \r <p>An individual or a director, officer, or agent of a\r corporation who knowingly and willfully violates<s> Section</s><ins> §</ins> 610 of\r the<s> National Manufactured Housing Construction and Safety Standards</s><ins>\r Federal</ins> Act in a manner<s> which</s><ins> that</ins> threatens the health or\r safety of any purchaser<s> shall be deemed</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a Class 1\r misdemeanor and<ins>,</ins> upon conviction<ins>, shall be</ins> fined not more than\r $1,000 or imprisoned not more than<s> one year</s><ins> 364 days</ins>, or both.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.32\">62.1-44.32</a>. (Effective until July 1, 2024) Penalties.</p>\r \r <p>(a) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who violates any provision of this chapter, or who fails, neglects, or refuses\r to comply with any order of the Board, or order of a court, issued as herein\r provided, shall be subject to a civil penalty not to exceed $32,500 for each\r violation within the discretion of the court. Each day of violation of each\r requirement shall constitute a separate offense. Such civil penalties shall be\r paid into the state treasury and deposited by the State Treasurer into the\r Virginia Environmental Emergency Response Fund pursuant to Chapter 25 of Title 10.1,\r excluding penalties assessed for violations of Article 9 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:8\">62.1-44.34:8</a> et\r seq.) or 10 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:10\">62.1-44.34:10</a> et seq.) of Chapter 3.1 of Title 62.1, or a\r regulation, administrative or judicial order, or term or condition of approval\r relating to or issued under those articles.</p>\r \r <p>Such civil penalties may, in the discretion of the court\r assessing them, be directed to be paid into the treasury of the county, city,\r or town in which the violation occurred, to be used for the purpose of abating\r environmental pollution therein in such manner as the court may, by order,\r direct, except that where the owner in violation is such county, city or town\r itself, or its agent, the court shall direct such penalty to be paid into the\r state treasury and deposited by the State Treasurer into the Virginia\r Environmental Emergency Response Fund pursuant to Chapter 25 of Title 10.1,\r excluding penalties assessed for violations of Article 9 or 10 of Chapter 3.1\r of Title 62.1, or a regulation, administrative or judicial order, or term or condition\r of approval relating to or issued under those articles.</p>\r \r <p>In the event that a county, city, or town, or its agent, is\r the owner, such county, city, or town, or its agent, may initiate a civil\r action against any user or users of a waste water treatment facility to recover\r that portion of any civil penalty imposed against the owner proximately\r resulting from the act or acts of such user or users in violation of any\r applicable federal, state, or local requirements.</p>\r \r <p>(b) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who willfully or negligently violates any provision of this chapter, any\r regulation or order of the Board, any condition of a certificate<ins>,</ins> or any\r order of a court<s> shall be</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a misdemeanor punishable\r by confinement in jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a\r fine of not less than $2,500 nor more than $32,500, either or both. Any person\r who knowingly violates any provision of this chapter, any regulation or order\r of the Board, any condition of a certificate<ins>,</ins> or any order of a court\r issued as herein provided, or who knowingly makes any false statement in any\r form required to be submitted under this chapter or knowingly renders\r inaccurate any monitoring device or method required to be maintained under this\r chapter,<s> shall be</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a felony punishable by a term of\r imprisonment of not less than one year nor more than three years, or in the\r discretion of the jury or the court trying the case without a jury, confinement\r in jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a fine of not\r less than $5,000 nor more than $50,000 for each violation. Any defendant that\r is not an individual shall, upon conviction of a violation under this\r subsection, be sentenced to pay a fine of not less than $10,000. Each day of\r violation of each requirement shall constitute a separate offense.</p>\r \r <p>(c) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who knowingly violates any provision of this chapter, and who knows at that\r time that he thereby places another person in imminent danger of death or\r serious bodily harm, shall, upon conviction, be guilty of a felony punishable\r by a term of imprisonment of not less than two years nor more than 15 years and\r a fine of not more than $250,000, either or both. A defendant that is not an\r individual shall, upon conviction of a violation under this subsection, be\r sentenced to pay a fine not exceeding the greater of $1 million or an amount\r that is three times the economic benefit realized by the defendant as a result\r of the offense. The maximum penalty shall be doubled with respect to both fine\r and imprisonment for any subsequent conviction of the same person under this\r subsection.</p>\r \r <p>(d) Criminal prosecution under this section shall be commenced\r within three years of discovery of the offense, notwithstanding the limitations\r provided in any other statute.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.32\">62.1-44.32</a>. (Effective July 1, 2024) Penalties.</p>\r \r <p>(a) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who violates any provision of this chapter, or who fails, neglects, or refuses\r to comply with any regulation, certificate, land-disturbance approval, or order\r of the Board, or order of a court, issued as herein provided, shall be subject\r to a civil penalty not to exceed $32,500 for each violation within the\r discretion of the court. Each day of violation of each requirement shall\r constitute a separate offense. Such civil penalties shall be paid into the\r state treasury and deposited by the State Treasurer into the Virginia\r Environmental Emergency Response Fund pursuant to Chapter 25 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-2500\">10.1-2500</a> et\r seq.) of Title 10.1, excluding penalties assessed for violations of Article 2.3\r (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.15:24\">62.1-44.15:24</a> et seq.), 2.4 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.15:51\">62.1-44.15:51</a> et seq.), 2.5 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.15:67\">62.1-44.15:67</a>\r et seq.), 9 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:8\">62.1-44.34:8</a> et seq.), or 10 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:10\">62.1-44.34:10</a> et seq.) of\r Chapter 3.1 of Title 62.1, or a regulation, administrative or judicial order,\r or term or condition of approval relating to or issued under those articles.</p>\r \r <p>Such civil penalties may, in the discretion of the court\r assessing them, be directed to be paid into the treasury of the county, city,\r or town in which the violation occurred, to be used for the purpose of abating\r environmental pollution therein in such manner as the court may, by order,\r direct, except that where the owner in violation is such county, city, or town\r itself, or its agent, the court shall direct such penalty to be paid into the\r state treasury and deposited by the State Treasurer into the Virginia\r Environmental Emergency Response Fund pursuant to Chapter 25 of Title 10.1,\r excluding penalties assessed for violations of Article 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 9, or 10\r of Chapter 3.1 of Title 62.1, or a regulation, administrative or judicial\r order, or term or condition of approval relating to or issued under those\r articles.</p>\r \r <p>In the event that a county, city, or town, or its agent, is\r the owner, such county, city, or town, or its agent, may initiate a civil\r action against any user or users of a waste water treatment facility to recover\r that portion of any civil penalty imposed against the owner proximately\r resulting from the act or acts of such user or users in violation of any\r applicable federal, state, or local requirements.</p>\r \r <p>(b) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who willfully or negligently violates (1) any provision of this chapter, any\r regulation or order of the Board, or any condition of a certificate or\r land-disturbance approval of the Board, (2) any land-disturbance approval,\r ordinance, or order of a locality serving as a Virginia Erosion and Stormwater\r Management Program authority, or (3) any order of a court shall be guilty of a\r misdemeanor punishable by confinement in jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins>\r 364 days</ins> and a fine of not less than $2,500 nor more than $32,500, either\r or both. Any person who knowingly violates (A) any provision of this chapter,\r any regulation or order of the Board, or any condition of a certificate or\r land-disturbance approval of the Board, (B) any land-disturbance approval,\r ordinance, or order of a locality serving as a Virginia Erosion and Stormwater\r Management Program authority, or (C) any order of a court issued as herein\r provided, or who knowingly makes any false statement in any form required to be\r submitted under this chapter or knowingly renders inaccurate any monitoring\r device or method required to be maintained under this chapter, shall be guilty\r of a felony punishable by a term of imprisonment of not less than one year nor\r more than three years, or in the discretion of the jury or the court trying the\r case without a jury, confinement in jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins>\r 364 days</ins> and a fine of not less than $5,000 nor more than $50,000 for each\r violation. Any defendant that is not an individual shall, upon conviction of a\r violation under this subsection, be sentenced to pay a fine of not less than\r $10,000. Each day of violation of each requirement shall constitute a separate\r offense.</p>\r \r <p>(c) Except as otherwise provided in this chapter, any person\r who knowingly violates any provision of this chapter, and who knows at that\r time that he thereby places another person in imminent danger of death or serious\r bodily harm, shall, upon conviction, be guilty of a felony punishable by a term\r of imprisonment of not less than two years nor more than 15 years and a fine of\r not more than $250,000, either or both. A defendant that is not an individual\r shall, upon conviction of a violation under this subsection, be sentenced to\r pay a fine not exceeding the greater of $1 million or an amount that is three\r times the economic benefit realized by the defendant as a result of the\r offense. The maximum penalty shall be doubled with respect to both fine and\r imprisonment for any subsequent conviction of the same person under this\r subsection.</p>\r \r <p>(d) Criminal prosecution under this section shall be commenced\r within three years of discovery of the offense, notwithstanding the limitations\r provided in any other statute.</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:20\">62.1-44.34:20</a>. Enforcement and penalties.</p>\r \r <p>A. Upon a finding of a violation of this article or a\r regulation or term or condition of approval issued pursuant to this article,\r the Board is authorized to issue a special order requiring any person to cease\r and desist from causing or permitting such violation or requiring any person to\r comply with any such provision, regulation or term or condition of approval.\r Such special orders shall be issued only after notice and an opportunity for\r hearing except that, if the Board finds that any discharge in violation of this\r article poses a serious threat to (i) the public health, safety or welfare or\r the health of animals, fish, botanic or aquatic life; (ii) a public water\r supply; or (iii) recreational, commercial, industrial, agricultural or other\r reasonable uses, the Board may issue, without advance notice or hearing, an\r emergency special order requiring the operator of any facility, vehicle or\r vessel to cease such discharge immediately, to implement any applicable\r contingency plan and to effect containment and cleanup. Such emergency special\r order may also require the operator of a facility to modify or cease regular\r operation of the facility, or any portion thereof, until the Board determines\r that continuing regular operation of the facility, or such portion thereof,\r will not pose a substantial threat of additional or continued discharges. The\r Board shall affirm, modify, amend or cancel any such emergency order after\r providing notice and opportunity for hearing to the operator charged with the\r violation. The notice of the hearing and the emergency order shall be issued at\r the same time. If an operator who has been issued such a special order or an\r emergency special order is not complying with the terms thereof, the Board may\r proceed in accordance with subsection B of this section, and where the order is\r based on a finding of an imminent and substantial danger, the court shall issue\r an injunction compelling compliance with the emergency special order pending a\r hearing by the Board. If an emergency special order requires modification or\r cessation of operations, the Board shall provide an opportunity for a hearing within\r 48 hours of the issuance of the injunction.</p>\r \r <p>B. In the event of a violation of this article or a\r regulation, administrative or judicial order, or term or condition of approval\r issued under this article, or in the event of failure to comply with a special\r order issued by the Board pursuant to this section, the Board is authorized to\r proceed by civil action to obtain an injunction of such violation, to obtain\r such affirmative equitable relief as is appropriate and to recover all costs,\r damages and civil penalties resulting from such violation or failure to comply.\r The Board shall be entitled to an award of reasonable attorneys\' fees and costs\r in any action in which it is a prevailing party.</p>\r \r <p>C. Any person who violates or causes or permits to be violated\r a provision of this article, or a regulation, administrative or judicial order,\r or term or condition of approval issued under this article, shall be subject to\r a civil penalty for each such violation as follows:</p>\r \r <p>1. For failing to obtain approval of an oil discharge\r contingency plan as required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:15\">62.1-44.34:15</a>, not less than $1,000 nor more\r than $50,000 for the initial violation, and $5,000 per day for each day of\r violation thereafter;</p>\r \r <p>2. For failing to maintain evidence of financial\r responsibility as required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:16\">62.1-44.34:16</a>, not less than $1,000 nor more\r than $100,000 for the initial violation, and $5,000 per day for each day of\r violation thereafter;</p>\r \r <p>3. For discharging or causing or permitting a discharge of oil\r into or upon state waters, or owning or operating any facility, vessel or\r vehicle from which such discharge originates in violation of § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:18\">62.1-44.34:18</a>,\r up to $100 per gallon of oil discharged;</p>\r \r <p>4. For failing to cooperate in containment and cleanup of a\r discharge as required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:18\">62.1-44.34:18</a> or for failing to report a discharge\r as required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:19\">62.1-44.34:19</a>, not less than $1,000 nor more than $50,000 for\r the initial violation, and $10,000 for each day of violation thereafter; and</p>\r \r <p>5. For violating or causing or permitting to be violated any\r other provision of this article, or a regulation, administrative or judicial\r order, or term or condition of approval issued under this article, up to\r $32,500 for each violation. Each day of violation of each requirement shall\r constitute a separate offense.</p>\r \r <p>D. Civil penalties may be assessed under this article either\r by a court in an action brought by the Board pursuant to this section, as\r specified in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.15\">62.1-44.15</a>, or with the consent of the person charged, in a\r special order issued by the Board. All penalties shall be paid into the state\r treasury and deposited by the State Treasurer into the Virginia Underground\r Petroleum Storage Tank Fund as established in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:11\">62.1-44.34:11</a>. In determining\r the amount of any penalty, consideration shall be given to the willfulness of\r the violation, any history of noncompliance, the actions of the person in\r reporting, containing and cleaning up any discharge or threat of discharge, the\r damage or injury to state waters or the impairment of their beneficial use, the\r cost of containment and cleanup, the nature and degree of injury to or\r interference with general health, welfare and property, and the available\r technology for preventing, containing, reducing or eliminating the discharge.</p>\r \r <p>E. Any person who knowingly violates, or causes or permits to\r be violated, a provision of this article, or a regulation, administrative or\r judicial order, or term or condition of approval issued under this article<s>\r shall be</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a misdemeanor punishable by confinement in\r jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins> 364 days</ins> and a fine of not more\r than $100,000, either or both. Any person who knowingly or willfully makes any\r false statement, representation or certification in any application, record,\r report, plan or other document filed or required to be maintained by this\r article or by administrative or judicial order issued under this article<s>\r shall be</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a felony punishable by a term of imprisonment\r of not less than one nor more than three years and a fine of not more than $100,000,\r either or both. In the case of a discharge of oil into or upon state waters:</p>\r \r <p>1. Any person who negligently discharges or negligently causes\r or permits such discharge<s> shall be</s><ins> is</ins> guilty of a misdemeanor\r punishable by confinement in jail for not more than<s> 12 months</s><ins> 364\r days</ins> and a fine of not more than $50,000, either or both.</p>\r \r <p>2. Any person who knowingly and willfully discharges or\r knowingly and willfully causes or permits such discharge<s> shall be</s><ins> is</ins>\r guilty of a felony punishable by a term of imprisonment of not less than one\r year nor more than 10 years and a fine of not more than $100,000, either or\r both.</p>\r \r <p>F. Each day of violation of each requirement shall constitute\r a separate offense. In the event the violation of this article follows a prior\r felony conviction under subdivision E 2 of this section, such violation shall\r constitute a felony and shall be punishable by a term of imprisonment of not\r less than two years nor more than 10 years and a fine of not more than\r $200,000, either or both.</p>\r \r <p>G. Upon conviction for a violation of any provision of this\r article, or a regulation, administrative or judicial order, or term or\r condition of approval issued under this article, a defendant who is not an\r individual shall be sentenced to pay a fine not exceeding the greater of:</p>\r \r <p>1. $1 million; or</p>\r \r <p>2. An amount that is three times the economic benefit, if any,\r realized by the defendant as a result of the offense.</p>\r \r <p>H. Any tank vessel entering upon state waters which fails to\r provide evidence of financial responsibility required by § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/62.1-44.34:16\">62.1-44.34:16</a>, and\r any vessel from which oil is discharged into or upon state waters, may be\r detained and held as security for payment to the Commonwealth of any damages or\r penalties assessed under this section. Such damages and penalties shall\r constitute a lien on the vessel and the lien shall secure all costs of\r containment and cleanup, damages, fines and penalties, as the case may be, for\r which the operator may be liable. The vessel shall be released upon posting of\r a bond with surety in the maximum amount of such damages or penalties.</p>\r \r <p> [ 2. That the\r Virginia Criminal Sentencing Commission shall revise all labels on Sentencing\r Guidelines worksheets and instructions in the Sentencing Guidelines Manual so\r that any conviction for an offense defined as a Class 1 misdemeanor or any\r other misdemeanor with a maximum penalty of 364 days of incarceration, as\r provided by the provisions of this act, shall be scored as if the maximum\r penalty were 12 months for the purposes of preparing and using the\r discretionary sentencing guidelines pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/19.2-298.01\">19.2-298.01</a> of the Code of\r Virginia. But the Commission shall not modify any scores assigned on the\r worksheets without following the process prescribed in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/17.1-806\">17.1-806</a> of the Code\r of Virginia. ] </p>','FE122',2,'house','failed committee','failed',NULL,'<p>According to the Fiscal Impact Statement, this legislation is not expected to have a fiscal impact on agency operations.</p><p>Summary generated automatically by OpenAI.</p>','8eb15c223afae771db7ef420a05710d0',96,0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-10','2024-11-17 00:55:11','2024-01-10 14:55:08');
