(74452,'hb1120',30,'house','K-12 schools and higher educational institutions; student participation in women\'s sports, etc.',519,'<b>K-12 schools and institutions of higher education; student participation in women\'s sports; civil cause of action.</b> Requires each interscholastic, intercollegiate, intramural, or club athletic team or sport sponsored by a public school, or any other school that is a member of the Virginia High School League, or by a public institution of higher education to be expressly designated as one of the following based on the biological sex of the students who participate on the team or in the sport: (i) males, men, or boys; (ii) females, women, or girls; or (iii) coed or mixed if participation on such team or sport is open to both males and females. The bill prohibits any such team or sport that is expressly designated for females from being open to students whose biological sex is male. The bill also provides that in the event of a dispute as to the biological sex of any student seeking to participate on any interscholastic, intercollegiate, intramural, or club athletic team or sport that is expressly designated for males or females, such student may establish biological sex by presenting to the school or institution a signed physician\'s statement that attests to such student\'s biological sex based solely on (a) the student\'s internal and external reproductive anatomy; (b) the student\'s normal endogenously produced levels of testosterone; and (c) an analysis of the student\'s genetic makeup.  The bill prohibits any government entity, licensing or accrediting organization, or athletic association or organization from entertaining a complaint, opening an investigation, or taking any other adverse action against any such school or institution of higher education based on a violation of the provisions of the bill and creates a cause of action for any school or institution of higher education that suffers harm as a result of a violation of the bill. Finally, the bill creates a civil cause of action for any student who suffers harm as a result of a knowing violation of a provision of the bill by a school or institution or as a result of the student\'s reporting a violation of a provision of the bill by a school, institution, athletic association, or organization.','HOUSE BILL NO. 1120\r </b></center>\r <center>\r Offered January 10, 2024\r </center>\r <center>\r Prefiled January 10, 2024\r </center>\r <i>A BILL to amend the Code of Virginia by adding sections \r  numbered <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-271.9\">22.1-271.9</a> and <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/23.1-408.2\">23.1-408.2</a>, relating to K-12 schools and institutions \r  of higher education; student \r  participation in women\'s sports; civil cause of action.</i>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Patron-- Oates\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Committee Referral Pending\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r  \r   <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r  \r   <p>1. That the Code of Virginia is amended by adding sections \r  numbered <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-271.9\">22.1-271.9</a> and <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/23.1-408.2\">23.1-408.2</a> as follows:</p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/22.1-271.9\">22.1-271.9</a>. \r  Interscholastic, intramural, and club athletic teams and sports; designation of \r  teams; student participation.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Each interscholastic, \r  intramural, or club athletic team or sport sponsored by a public school or any \r  other school that is a member of the Virginia High School League shall \r  be expressly designated as one of the following based on the \r  biological sex of the students who participate on the team or in \r  the sport:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. For \"males,\" \r  \"men,\" or \"boys\";</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. For \r  \"females,\" \"women,\" or \"girls\"; or</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. For \"coed\" or \r  \"mixed\" if participation on such team or in such sport is open to \r  both (i) males, men, or boys and (ii) females, women, or girls.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. Each interscholastic, \r  intramural, or club athletic team or sport sponsored by a public \r  school or any other school \r  that is a member of the Virginia High School League that is \r  expressly designated for \"females,\" \"women,\" \r  or \"girls,\" pursuant to subsection A shall not be open to \r  any student of the male sex.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. If the \r  biological sex of any student seeking to participate on such an expressly \r  designated team is disputed, such student may establish biological sex by \r  presenting to the school administration or such other \r  authority designated by the school for such \r  purpose a signed physician\'s statement that shall attest to the student\'s \r  biological sex based solely on:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. The student\'s internal \r  and external reproductive anatomy;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. The student\'s normal \r  endogenously produced levels of testosterone; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. An analysis of the \r  student\'s genetic makeup.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>D. No \r  government entity, licensing or accrediting organization, or athletic association \r  or organization shall entertain a complaint, open an investigation, or take \r  any other adverse action against a school for \r  explicitly designating or maintaining separate interscholastic, intramural, or club \r  athletic teams or sports for \"females,\" \"women,\" or \"girls\" \r  pursuant to subsections A, B, and C. Any \r  school that suffers any direct or indirect \r  harm as a result of a violation of this section shall \r  have a private cause of action for injunctive relief, damages, and any other \r  relief available under law against such government entity, licensing or accrediting \r  organization, or athletic association or organization.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>E. Any \r  student who is (i) deprived of an athletic opportunity or suffers any direct \r  or indirect harm as a result of a school knowingly violating this section \r  or (ii) subject to retaliation or other adverse action by a school, athletic \r  association, or organization as a result of reporting a violation of this \r  section to an employee or representative \r  of such school, athletic association, or organization, or to any state or \r  federal agency with oversight of such school \r  in the Commonwealth, shall have a \r  private cause of action for injunctive relief, damages, and any other relief \r  available under law against such school, athletic association, or organization.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>F. The provisions of \r  subsections D and E shall constitute a waiver \r  of sovereign immunity.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>G. All \r  civil actions brought pursuant to subsection D or E must be \r  initiated within two years after the harm occurred. Any \r  person who prevails on a claim brought pursuant to this section \r  shall be entitled to monetary damages, including for any psychological, \r  emotional, or physical harm suffered, reasonable attorney fees and costs, and \r  any other appropriate relief.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/23.1-408.2\">23.1-408.2</a>. \r  Intercollegiate, intramural, and club athletic teams and sports; designation of \r  teams; student participation.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Each \r  intercollegiate, intramural, and club athletic team or sport that is sponsored \r  by a public institution of higher education or any institution of \r  higher education that is a member of the National Collegiate Athletic \r  Association (NCAA), National Association of Intercollegiate Athletics \r  (NAIA), or National Junior College Athletic Association (NJCAA) shall \r  be expressly designated as one of the \r  following based on the biological sex of the \r  students who participate on the team or in the sport:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. For \"males,\" \r  \"men,\" or \"boys\";</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. For \r  \"females,\" \"women,\" or \"girls\"; \r  or</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. For \"coed\" or \r  \"mixed,\" if participation on such \r  team or sport is open to both (i) males, men, or \r  boys and (ii) females, women, or girls.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. Each \r  intercollegiate, intramural, or club athletic team or sport that is sponsored \r  by any such institution of higher education and that is expressly designated \r  for \"females,\" \"women,\" or \"girls\" pursuant \r  to subsection A shall not be open to any student of the \r  male sex.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. If the \r  biological sex of any such student seeking to participate on such an expressly \r  designated team is disputed, such student may establish biological sex by \r  presenting to the administration of the institution, or such \r  other authority designated by such \r  institution for such purpose, a \r  signed physician\'s statement that shall attest to the student\'s biological sex \r  based solely on:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. The student\'s internal \r  and external reproductive anatomy;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. The student\'s normal endogenously \r  produced levels of testosterone; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. An analysis of the \r  student\'s genetic makeup.<s> </s></ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>D. No \r  government entity, licensing or accrediting organization, or athletic association \r  or organization shall entertain a complaint, open an investigation, or take \r  any other adverse action against an institution of higher education for \r  explicitly designating or maintaining \r  separate intercollegiate, intramural, or club athletic teams or sports \r  for \"females, \"women,\" or \"girls\" pursuant to subsections \r  A, B, and C. Any \r  institution of higher education that \r  suffers any direct or indirect harm as \r  a result of a violation of this section shall have a private cause of action \r  for injunctive relief, damages, and any other relief available under law against \r  such government entity, licensing or accrediting organization, or athletic \r  association or organization.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>E. Any \r  student who is (i) deprived of an athletic opportunity or suffers any direct \r  or indirect harm as a result of a knowing violation of this section by any such \r  institution of higher education or \r  (ii) subject to retaliation or other adverse action by an \r  institution of higher education, athletic association, or organization as a \r  result of reporting a violation of this section to an employee \r  or representative of such institution, athletic association, or organization, \r  or to any state or federal agency with oversight of such institution \r  in the Commonwealth, shall have a private cause of action for injunctive \r  relief, damages, and any other \r  relief available under law against such institution, athletic association, \r  or organization.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>F. The provisions of \r  subsections D and E shall constitute a waiver \r  of sovereign immunity.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>G. All \r  civil actions brought pursuant to subsection \r  D or E must be \r  initiated within two years after the \r  harm occurred. Any person who prevails on a claim brought \r  pursuant to this section shall be entitled to monetary damages, including for \r  any psychological, emotional, and physical harm suffered, reasonable attorney \r  fees and costs, and any other appropriate relief.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p>2. That the General Assembly finds that (i) there are \r  \"inherent differences between men and women,\" and that these \r  differences \"remain cause for celebration, but not for denigration of the \r  members of either sex or artificial constraints on an individual\'s \r  opportunity\" (<ins>United States v. Virginia</ins> 518 U.S. 515, 533 (1996)), \r  and these \"inherent differences\" range \r  from chromosomal and hormonal differences to physiological differences, \r  including men generally have \"denser, stronger bones, tendons, and ligaments,\" \r  \"larger hearts, greater lung volume per body mass, a higher red blood cell \r  count, and higher hemoglobin\" (Neel Burton, \"The \r  Battle of the Sexes,\" <ins>Psychology Today</ins> \r  (July 2, 2012)), higher natural levels of \r  testosterone, which affects traits such as body fat content, the storage and \r  use of carbohydrates, and the development of type two muscle fibers, all of \r  which result in men being able to generate higher speed and power during \r  physical activity (Doriane Lambelet Coleman, \"Sex \r  in Sport,\" <ins>Law and Contemporary Problems</ins> \r  63, 74 (2017)); (ii) that these biological differences between men and women \r  \"explain the male and female secondary sex characteristics which develop \r  during puberty and have lifelong effects, including those most important for \r  success in sport: categorically different strength, speed and endurance\" (Doriane Lambelet Coleman and Wickliffe \r  Shreve, \"Comparing Athletic Performances: The \r  Best Elite Women to Boys and Men,\" Duke Law \r  Center for Sports Law and Policy), and that while classifications based on sex \r  are generally disfavored, the Supreme Court has recognized that \"sex \r  classifications may be used to compensate women for particular economic \r  disabilities [they have] suffered, to promote equal employment opportunity, \r  [and] to advance full development of the talent and capacities of our Nation\'s \r  people\" (<ins>United States v. Virginia</ins>, 518 U.S. 515, 533 (1996)); \r  (iii) that one place in which sex classifications allow for the \"full \r  development of the talent and capacities of our Nation\'s people\" is in the \r  context of sports and athletics (<ins>Id.</ins>); (iv) that courts have recognized \r  that the inherent, physiological differences between males and females result \r  in different athletic capabilities, as seen in <ins>Kleczek \r  v. Rhode Island Interscholastic League, Inc.</ins>, 612 A.2d 734, 738 (R.I. 1992):\"Because of innate physiological differences, boys \r  and girls are not similarly situated as they enter athletic competition.\" and in <ins>Petrie v. Illinois High Sch. Ass\'n</ins>, \r  394 N.E. 2d 855, 861 (Ill. Ct. App. 1979): finding \r  that \"high school boys [generally possess \r  physiological advantages over] their girl counterparts\" and that those \r  advantages give them an unfair lead over girls in some sports like \"high \r  school track\"; (v) that a recent study of female and male Olympic performances \r  dating back to 1983 found that, although athletes of both sexes improved over \r  the time span, the \"gender gap\" between female and male performances \r  remains stable and \"[t]h[is] suggest[s] that women\'s performances at the \r  high level will never match those of men\" (Valerie Thibault et al., \"Women and Men in Sport Performance: The Gender Gap \r  Has Not Evolved since 1983,\" <ins>Journal of \r  Sports Science &amp; Medicine</ins> 214, 291 (2010)); \r  (vi) that, as Duke Law professor and All-American track athlete Doriane \r  Coleman, tennis champion Martina Navratilova, and Olympic track gold medalist \r  Sanya Richards-Ross wrote, \"[t]he evidence is unequivocal that starting in \r  puberty, in every sport except sailing, shooting, and riding, there will always \r  be significant numbers of boys and men who would beat the best girls and women \r  in head-to-head competition. Claims to the contrary are simply a denial of \r  science.\" (Doriane Coleman, Martina Navratilova, et al., \"Pass the Equality Act, But Don\'t Abandon Title IX,\" <ins>Washington Post</ins> (Apr. 29, 2019)); and (vii) \r  that the benefits that natural testosterone provides to male athletes is not \r  diminished through the use of puberty blockers and cross-sex hormones. A recent \r  study on the impact of such treatments found that even \"after 12 months of \r  hormonal therapy\" a man who identifies as a woman and is taking cross-sex \r  hormones \"had an absolute advantage\" over female athletes and \r  \"will still likely have performance benefits\" over women (Tommy \r  Lundberg, et al., \"Muscle \r  strength, size and composition following months of gender-affirming treatment \r  of transgender individuals: retained advantage for the transwomen,\" Karolinska Institute (Sept. 26, 2019)); and that \r  the General Assembly finds that having sex-specific teams provide opportunities \r  for female athletes to demonstrate their skill, strength, and athletic \r  abilities while also providing them with opportunities to obtain recognition \r  and accolades, college scholarships, and the numerous other long-term benefits \r  that flow from success in athletic endeavors, and that, therefore, maintaining \r  separate sex-specific teams is imperative to promoting sex equality and \r  necessary to ensuring female athletes equal protection under the law and equal \r  treatment and opportunity in public school and higher education athletics.</p>',NULL,6,'house','failed committee','failed',NULL,NULL,'392379fc48bee8b757cd251224de4585',100,0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-10','2024-11-01 06:51:45','2024-01-10 19:55:05');
