INSERT INTO `bills` VALUES
(73480,'hb457',30,'house','Decreasing probation period; establishes criteria for mandatory reduction.',497,'<b>Decreasing probation period; criteria for mandatory reduction; report.</b> Establishes criteria for which a defendant\'s supervised probation period shall be reduced, including completing qualifying educational activities, maintaining verifiable employment, and complying with or completing any state-certified or state-approved mental health or substance abuse treatment program. The bill provides that a court may decrease a defendant\'s probation period if warranted by the defendant\'s conduct and in the interests of justice and may do so without a hearing. The bill also directs the Department of Corrections to meet with relevant stakeholders and provide to the General Assembly by November 1, 2024, a report regarding certain probation practices. The provisions of the bill, other than the requirement that the Department submit a report to the General Assembly, are subject to reenactment by the 2025 Session of the General Assembly. This bill is identical to SB 80.','<br><center><b>VIRGINIA ACTS OF ASSEMBLY -- CHAPTER</b></center>\r \r <i>An Act to amend and reenact § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/19.2-304\">19.2-304</a> of the Code of\r Virginia, relating to decreasing probation period; criteria for mandatory\r reduction; report.</i>\r \r <center>[H 457]</center>\r \r <center>Approved</center>\r \r <p> </p>\r \r <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r \r <p>1. That § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/19.2-304\">19.2-304</a> of the Code of Virginia is amended and\r reenacted as follows:</p>\r \r <p>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/19.2-304\">19.2-304</a>. Increasing or decreasing probation period and\r modification of conditions; mandatory reduction in probation period.</p>\r \r <p><ins>A. </ins>The court may subsequently increase<s> or decrease</s>\r the probation period and may revoke or modify any condition of probation<s>,</s>\r but only upon a hearing after reasonable notice to both the defendant and the\r attorney for the Commonwealth.<ins> After fixing the probation period, the court\r may subsequently decrease the probation period without a hearing if warranted\r by the defendant\'s conduct and in the interests of justice.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. The defendant\'s supervised probation period shall be\r reduced if:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. The defendant completes a qualifying educational\r activity, including obtaining a high school diploma, passing high school\r equivalency testing, completing 15 credit hours with at least a 2.0 grade point\r average at a postsecondary educational institution, obtaining an academic\r degree, completing a peer recovery specialist training program approved by the\r Department of Behavioral Health and Developmental Services, completing a\r state-certified training program, or completing a vocational or job training program\r made available by the Department of Corrections or by a community provider\r approved by the Department of Corrections;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. The defendant maintains verifiable employment where the\r defendant is employed at least an average of 30 hours per week; or</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. The defendant is in compliance with any state-certified\r or state-approved mental health or substance abuse treatment program or\r successfully completes a state-certified or state-approved mental health or\r substance abuse treatment program.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>C. A defendant shall not begin accruing credit for any\r educational activity, employment, or treatment program described in subsection\r B until the first day of the first full month that such defendant is on\r supervised probation. A defendant may accrue credits from each of subdivisions\r B 1, 2, and 3 in each six-month reporting period. Such credits shall not accrue\r during any calendar month in which the defendant is found in violation of his\r supervised probation, unless such violation is a technical violation and the\r probation officer for such defendant determines that there exists a compelling\r reason to award credits for such period of time. For every six-month period of\r qualifying educational activity the defendant completes in accordance with\r subdivision B 1, the defendant\'s supervised probation period shall be reduced\r by 60 days. For every six-month period of verifiable employment the defendant\r completes in accordance with subdivision B 2, the defendant\'s supervised\r probation period shall be reduced by 30 days. For every six-month period of\r qualifying mental health or substance abuse treatment program compliance or for\r completion of a qualifying mental health or substance abuse treatment program\r in accordance with subdivision B 3, the defendant\'s supervised probation period\r shall be reduced by 60 days.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A defendant\'s probation officer shall promptly and no less\r than quarterly calculate any reductions to such defendant\'s supervised\r probation for any qualifying evidence-based recidivism-reducing activities,\r including completing qualifying educational activities, maintaining verifiable\r employment, and completing or complying with any state-certified or\r state-approved mental health or substance abuse treatment program under this\r subsection. A defendant\'s probation officer shall verify employment through supporting\r documentation, which may include any record, employer letter, pay stub,\r contract, or other approved method of verification by the probation services\r agency or the Department of Corrections. A defendant\'s probation officer shall\r verify the education and vocation compliance or completion through supporting\r documentation, which may include any record, facilitator or program letter,\r report card, progress report, or other approved method of verification by the\r probation services agency or Department of Corrections. A defendant\'s probation\r officer shall verify the mental health or substance abuse treatment program\r compliance or completion through supporting documentation, which may include\r any record, facilitator or program letter, progress report, sign-in sheet, or\r other approved method of verification by the probation services agency or the\r Department of Corrections. A decision by the defendant\'s probation officer\r regarding the qualification of any educational activity, employment, or\r treatment program described in subsection B is final.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>When the accumulation of time served on probation and any\r earned reduction is equal to the imposed supervised probation term, and the\r defendant has satisfied all nonmonetary conditions of his supervised probation,\r including any court-ordered programming or community service hours, the\r probation officer shall notify the court of the defendant\'s reduction in his\r probation term under this subsection. Upon receipt of this information, the\r court shall enter an order discharging the person from supervised probation.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>D. The provisions of this section shall not apply to any\r person sentenced pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/19.2-303.3\">19.2-303.3</a> and subject to supervised probation\r by a local community-based probation services agency established pursuant to\r Article 9 (§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/9.1-173\">9.1-173</a> et seq.) of Chapter 1 of Title 9.1.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>E. No defendant shall have his probation reduced pursuant\r to this section for his participation in any court-ordered treatment.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>F. Nothing in this section shall be construed to limit the\r court\'s ability to reduce a defendant\'s period of probation or discharge such\r defendant from probation under any other provision of law.</ins></p>\r \r <p>2. That the provisions of the first enactment of this act\r shall not become effective unless reenacted by the 2025 Session of the General\r Assembly.</p>\r \r <p>3. That the Department of Corrections shall meet with all\r relevant stakeholders, including the Virginia Probation and Parole Association\r and criminal justice reform organizations, and provide to the General Assembly\r by November 1, 2024, a report that describes in detail (i) current practices\r for community supervision as it relates to monitoring engagement and attainment\r in education, employment, treatment, and other programs and making\r recommendations to the court for modification of time served on probation and\r (ii) how such practices compare to the processes and practices that would be\r established pursuant to the first enactment of this act if reenacted by the\r 2025 Session of the General Assembly.</p>','FH2122',2,'house','vetoed by governor','failed',NULL,'<p>This <a href=\"https://lis.virginia.gov/cgi-bin/legp604.exe?241+oth+HB457FH1122+PDF\">fiscal impact statement</a> does not provide an exact cost estimate for the legislation. However, it states that if the bill were to require additional Probation and Parole officers, funding of $76,811 general fund (includes salary and benefits) per officer would be required. The statement also mentions that the Office of the Attorney General may experience a fiscal impact due to potential litigation cases filed under the proposed bill, but the impact cannot be quantified at this time.</p>\n        <p class=\"openai\">Summary generated automatically by OpenAI.</p>','159dcf23ffbb35eedd2aad349380dba2',99,0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-08','2024-11-17 00:55:05','2024-01-08 21:54:03');
