INSERT INTO `bills_full_text` VALUES
(1027892,73984,'HB745',30,'2024-01-09','HOUSE BILL NO. 745\r </b></center>\r <center>\r Offered January 10, 2024\r </center>\r <center>\r Prefiled January 9, 2024\r </center>\r <i>A BILL to amend the Code of Virginia by adding in Chapter 14 \r  of Title 10.1 an article numbered 3.8, consisting of sections numbered <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a> \r  through <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.46\">10.1-1425.46</a>, relating to mattress stewardship program established; \r  civil penalty.</i>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Patron-- Laufer\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r <center>\r Committee Referral Pending\r </center>\r <center>----------</center>\r  \r   <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r  \r   <p>1. That the Code of Virginia is amended by adding in Chapter \r  14 of Title 10.1 an article numbered 3.8, consisting of sections numbered \r  <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a> through <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.46\">10.1-1425.46</a>, as follows:</p>\r  \r   <center><ins>Article 3.8.</ins></center>\r  \r   <center><ins>Mattress Stewardship Program.</ins></center>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a>. Definitions.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>As used in this article, \r  unless the context requires a different meaning:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Mattress\" means \r  a resilient material or combination of materials that is enclosed by a ticking, \r  is used alone or in combination with other products, and is intended for or \r  promoted for sleeping on. \"Mattress\" includes box \r  springs and any used or renovated materials. \"Mattress\" does not \r  include:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. An unattached mattress pad \r  or topper that is designed to be used on top of or in addition to a mattress;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. A waterbed, air mattress, \r  or any other product that contains liquid-filled or \r  gas-filled ticking and that does not contain upholstery material between the \r  ticking and the mattress core;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. A carriage, basket, \r  dressing table, stroller, playpen, infant carrier, lounge pad, crib bumper, \r  crib mattress, bassinet mattress, or any other product manufactured for young \r  children; or</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>4. A sleeping \r  bag, pillow, futon, sleeper sofa, fold-out sofa bed, mattress foundation, \r  car bed, or any furniture that otherwise does not contain a detachable \r  mattress.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Mattress core\" means the \r  principal support system that is present in a mattress, including \r  springs, foam, an air bladder, a water bladder, or resilient filling.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Mattress stewardship \r  assessment\" means the amount added to the purchase price \r  of each mattress sold in the \r  Commonwealth that is necessary to cover the mattress stewardship program\'s \r  cost of collecting, transporting, and processing \r  postconsumer mattresses within the Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Mattress stewardship \r  program\" means the program to collect \r  postconsumer mattresses established in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a>.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Mattress waste \r  management entity\" means a municipal, private, or \r  nonprofit waste management entity authorized or contracted by \r  the mattress stewardship program to collect, \r  disaggregate, reduce, reuse, and recycle mattresses.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Postconsumer \r  mattress\" means a mattress no longer wanted by a user. \r  \"Postconsumer mattress\" includes any unused \r  mattress and any mattress \r  that has been used and abandoned or discarded.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Producer\" means (i) a \r  manufacturer of a mattress that sells, offers for sale, or distributes \r  mattresses in the Commonwealth under the producer\'s own name or brand or (ii) an \r  individual that imports mattresses into the Commonwealth to sell, offer for \r  sale, or distribute in the Commonwealth. \"Producer\" includes \r  the owner of a mattress trademark or brand.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Renovate\" means to \r  alter a discarded mattress for resale through replacing the ticking or filling, \r  adding additional filling, or replacing components of the discarded \r  mattress with new or recycled materials. \"Renovate\" does not include \r  (i) stripping a discarded mattress of the ticking or \r  filling without adding new material or (ii) the sanitization or sterilization \r  of a discarded mattress without other alteration to the discarded mattress.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Representative \r  organization\" means a nonprofit organization created by producers to \r  implement a mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Retailer\" means any \r  person that offers mattresses for sale at retail in the Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Sale\" or \r  \"sell\" means any transfer of title for consideration, including \r  remote sales conducted through sales outlets, catalogues, or the Internet or \r  any other similar electronic means.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Sanitization\" means \r  the direct application of chemicals to a mattress to kill pathogens that cause \r  human disease.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>\"Sterilization\" \r  means the mitigation of any deleterious substances or organisms, including \r  pathogens that cause human disease, fungi, and insects, from a mattress or \r  filling using a chemical or heat process.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ \r  <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.41\">10.1-1425.41</a>. Disposal of mattresses in landfills prohibited.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Beginning January 1, \r  2028, no person shall dispose of \r  a mattress in a landfill, unless such \r  mattress is deeply contaminated and cannot be \r  renovated and no parts of such mattress can be recycled.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. Beginning January 1, \r  2028, no person shall dispose of a mattress in an \r  incinerator.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a>. Mattress \r  stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Beginning July 1, 2025, \r  producers of mattresses sold at retail in the Commonwealth, or a representative \r  organization acting on a producer\'s behalf, shall submit a plan for the \r  establishment of a mattress stewardship program to the Department \r  for approval. Such plan shall:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. Identify the materials, \r  entities, and responsible parties covered under the plan;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. Describe the organizational \r  structure of any representative organization;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. Describe \r  the financing method for the plan, including how fees will be structured and \r  collected;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>4. Describe sound management \r  practices that will be used for worker health and safety;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>5. Describe how discarded \r  mattresses not covered under the program will be handled;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>6. Describe how producers \r  will:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>a. Work with existing \r  recycling programs and infrastructure;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>b. Consult with state and \r  local governments and other stakeholders; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>c. Conduct research as needed \r  to improve the collection and recycling of mattresses;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>7. Describe how mattress waste \r  management entities will be solicited, recruited, and supported, including:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>a. Existing municipal, \r  private, and nonprofit entities in the Commonwealth that work in the mattress \r  waste stream management field;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>b. Existing \r  entities in the Commonwealth that work in the general waste management field;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>c. Entities that have \r  experience in or prioritize disaggregation, reuse, and \r  recycling of component mattress parts;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>d. Minority business \r  enterprises;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>e. Minority-owned businesses \r  that are not designated as minority business enterprises;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>f. Woman-owned businesses;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>g. Veteran-owned businesses; \r  and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>h. Businesses that prioritize \r  the training and hiring of individuals who are (i) reentering \r  society after imprisonment, (ii) experiencing \r  homelessness and housing insecurity, (iii) in \r  addiction recovery, (iv) \r  disabled, or (v) \r  otherwise socioeconomically or historically marginalized or disadvantaged;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>8. Provide an estimated annual \r  operating budget;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>9. Provide a plan for market \r  development;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>10. Provide the time frame for \r  achieving zero-waste goals and program profitability and sustainability without \r  the need for fees by:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>a. Encouraging the expansion \r  and development of downstream markets for disaggregated component mattress \r  parts;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>b. Increasing the efficiency \r  of end-of-life product management; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>c. Encouraging effective coordination \r  and sharing of resources where possible; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>11. Provide a plan in case of \r  closure of the program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. The plan pursuant to subsection \r  A shall minimize public sector involvement in, and financial responsibility for, \r  the management of mattress disposal by (i) promoting mattress reuse and \r  recycling and (ii) negotiating and executing agreements to collect, \r  transport, reuse, recycle, and dispose of mattresses and process mattresses for \r  materials recovery.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. The plan pursuant to subsection \r  A shall provide for the convenient and available \r  collection of mattresses in the Commonwealth by:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. Providing for convenient \r  and free access to collection or drop-off locations for \r  mattresses from residential, commercial, institutional, or other sources;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. Providing a convenient \r  method for the public to access a list of collection or drop-off locations;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. Identifying each producer \r  participating in the mattress stewardship program and the brands of mattresses \r  sold in the Commonwealth that are covered by the program;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>4. Providing geographic \r  modeling to determine the number and distribution of sites for all collection \r  of postconsumer mattresses based on the following criteria:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>a. At least 90 percent of the \r  residents of the Commonwealth shall have a collection site within a 15-mile radius; \r  and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>b. Unless otherwise approved \r  by the Department, one additional collection site shall be established for \r  every 50,000 residents of a geographical area designated under federal law as \r  an urbanized area;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>5. Providing information on \r  how mattresses contaminated by biological fluids, toxins, or infectious agents \r  will be disposed of, including whether such disposal will be handled inside or \r  outside of the Commonwealth; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>6. Allowing for the scheduling \r  and pickup of mattresses from \r  private property.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>D. The plan pursuant to \r  subsection A may identify a retailer as a postconsumer mattress collection site \r  if:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. Such retailer volunteers to \r  act as a postconsumer mattress collection site;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. Such retailer is in \r  compliance with all applicable laws and regulations; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. The site location of such \r  retailer is consistent with maintaining a convenient and available \r  network of postconsumer mattress collection locations.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>E. The plan pursuant to \r  subsection A shall include:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. A uniform mattress \r  stewardship assessment for all mattresses sold in the Commonwealth \r  and a mechanism for producers participating in a mattress stewardship program \r  to remit to the representative organization payment of the mattress stewardship \r  assessment for each mattress sold in the Commonwealth; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. Performance goals for:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>a. The collection rates of \r  discarded mattresses;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>b. The recycling rates of \r  discarded mattresses, including closed-loop recycling;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>c. The renovation rates of \r  discarded mattresses;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>d. The number of illegally \r  dumped mattresses and the share of illegally dumped \r  mattresses as a percentage of all mattresses in the mattress stewardship \r  program; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>e. Public awareness of the \r  mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>F. Each plan \r  submitted pursuant to subsection A (i) shall be reevaluated by the \r  Department every five years and (ii) may, if required by the Department, be \r  revised before the end of such five-year time period if targets \r  are not being met or a change in circumstances warrants a revision.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>G. The total \r  amount of the mattress stewardship assessment established in subsection E shall \r  not exceed the costs of implementing the mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>H. The \r  mattress stewardship assessment established in subsection E shall be evaluated by \r  an independent financial auditor, as designated by the Department, to ensure that the costs of \r  implementing the mattress stewardship program are covered but not exceeded. The cost of \r  such evaluation performed by an independent \r  financial auditor shall be funded by the mattress stewardship program. Mattress \r  stewardship assessments shall be (i) used only to \r  implement the mattress stewardship program and (ii) phased out when the \r  mattress stewardship program becomes profitable based on any products it \r  produces from the recycled material.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>I. The Department shall review \r  (i) the mattress stewardship program plan required by subsection A and \r  (ii) the work product of the independent financial auditor designated by the \r  Department pursuant to subsection H to evaluate \r  mattress stewardship assessments.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>J. The producer or \r  representative organization that submits a plan for approval shall pay a plan \r  review fee to the Department that covers the Department\'s \r  cost of plan review, including associated costs for mattress \r  stewardship program compliance oversight, as determined by the Department.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>K. The Department \r  shall (i) approve a mattress stewardship program if the Department \r  determines that the mattress stewardship program plan, including the mattress stewardship \r  assessment, complies with the requirements of subsection A and (ii) list on its \r  website the producers and brands implementing or participating in an approved \r  mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>L. A producer or \r  representative organization shall implement its mattress stewardship program plan within \r  one year after the plan\'s approval by the Department. Beginning one year after \r  a producer\'s or representative \r  organization\'s plan is approved:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. No producer or \r  retailer shall sell or offer for sale a brand of mattress to any \r  person in the Commonwealth unless such producer of the brand or a \r  representative organization of which the producer is a member is implementing \r  an approved mattress stewardship program;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. A producer shall add the \r  mattress stewardship assessment established under an approved mattress \r  stewardship program to the cost of all mattresses sold to retailers in the Commonwealth; \r  and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. Each retailer shall add a \r  mattress stewardship assessment to the purchase price of all mattresses sold in \r  the Commonwealth by displaying a line item on the receipt for the added cost.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>M. A producer or \r  representative organization participating in an approved mattress stewardship \r  program shall provide consumers with educational materials regarding such program \r  that include (i) information regarding available end-of-life \r  management options for mattresses offered through such program and \r  (ii) information that notifies consumers that an assessment to cover the costs \r  of implementing such program is included in the purchase \r  price of all mattresses sold in the Commonwealth. Following \r  the implementation of the mattress stewardship program, a retailer shall \r  provide consumers, at the point of retail sale, with educational materials \r  provided by a producer or representative organization on available collection \r  opportunities for postconsumer mattresses through such program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>N. Following the \r  implementation of the mattress stewardship program, a retailer shall be \r  considered compliant with the requirements of this section if, on the \r  date a mattress is ordered from the producer, the producer of such mattress is \r  listed on the Department\'s website as implementing or participating in an \r  approved mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>O. No \r  postconsumer mattress collection site that is identified in a \r  mattress stewardship program plan shall charge any additional \r  fee for the disposal of mattresses when mattresses are \r  offered for disposal, reuse, or recycling.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>P. A producer or representative \r  organization that organizes the collection, transportation, and processing of \r  mattresses in accordance with an approved mattress stewardship program shall be \r  immune from liability for any claim of a violation of antitrust, restraint of \r  trade, or unfair trade practice arising from conduct undertaken in accordance \r  with such program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.43\">10.1-1425.43</a>. Annual \r  reporting requirements.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Beginning March 1, 2027, or \r  one year after approval of a mattress stewardship program plan by \r  the Department, whichever is later, and annually thereafter, the producer or \r  representative organization shall submit to the Department \r  and publish online a report that details its mattress stewardship program, including:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. A description of the \r  methods used to collect, transport, and process mattresses in the Commonwealth;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. The volume and type of \r  mattresses collected in the Commonwealth sorted by the type of collection site;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. The volume and type of \r  mattresses collected in the Commonwealth by methods of \r  disposition, including reuse, recycling, and other methods of processing or \r  disposal, which includes an accounting of the volume of mattresses collected in \r  the Commonwealth for each locality;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>4. The total cost of \r  implementing and administering such mattress stewardship program, as determined \r  by an independent financial audit funded by the mattress stewardship \r  assessment;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>5. Samples of educational \r  materials used to inform consumers of mattress disposal, a summary of public \r  education efforts, and an evaluation of the effectiveness of such \r  educational materials and public education efforts;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>6. An evaluation of why unrecycled mattresses \r  were not recycled;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>7. The total number of \r  mattresses sold to consumers in the Commonwealth in the \r  previous year;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>8. Strategies to address \r  postconsumer and discarded mattresses that were not \r  included in such program and that were illegally \r  dumped;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>9. Reports on research \r  activities and pilot programs;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>10. A discussion of any \r  changes in mattress design;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>11. The number of \r  postconsumer mattresses received through collection that were not included in such program;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>12. The number of postconsumer \r  mattresses that were illegally dumped, as reported \r  to the Department;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>13. An analysis \r  of how the data required by this subsection has changed over time;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>14. An evaluation of the \r  effectiveness of methods and processes used to achieve the goals of such program, \r  including information on progress made toward achieving such goals, an \r  explanation of why any goals were not met during the previous calendar year, \r  and information on any efforts that will be \r  taken to improve progress toward meeting such goals in \r  the future, if applicable;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>15. Recommendations for any \r  changes to such program, including \r  information relevant to compliance with the plan; and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>16. Any other information \r  required by the Department.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. The producer or \r  representative organization that submits an annual report \r  required under subsection A shall pay a report review fee \r  to the Department that covers the Department\'s cost to \r  review such report, including associated costs for program compliance \r  oversight, as determined by the Department.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. The Department shall review \r  the annual report required under subsection A and (i) evaluate the total costs \r  of the mattress stewardship program, including all expenses and revenues, to \r  determine whether the mattress stewardship assessment meets or exceeds the \r  costs of the program in accordance with subsection G of § \r  <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a> and (ii) determine whether the program is being implemented in accordance \r  with the plan approved under subsection K of § \r  <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a>. If the Department determines that the mattress \r  stewardship annual report, including the mattress stewardship assessment, \r  complies with the requirements of subsection A, the Department shall \r  approve the annual report. The producer or representative organization shall \r  make the approved annual report available to the public. If the Department \r  determines that the mattress stewardship annual report, including the mattress \r  stewardship assessment, does not comply with the requirements of subsection A, the \r  producer or representative organization shall submit to the Department a \r  plan on how such producer or representative \r  organization will address the noncompliance in the upcoming year.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>D. Financial, production, or \r  sales data reported to the Department by a producer or representative \r  organization shall be kept confidential by the Department and shall not be \r  subject to public inspection. The Department may release summary data that does \r  not disclose financial, production, or sales data of a producer, retailer, or \r  representative organization.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.44\">10.1-1425.44</a>. Mattress \r  Stewardship Program Advisory Board established; \r  membership; duties and responsibilities; meetings; \r  annual report.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. There is hereby \r  established the Mattress Stewardship Program Advisory \r  Board, referred to in this section as \"the Advisory \r  Board,\" for the purpose of advising the Department on the \r  proper implementation and sustainability of the mattress stewardship program. The \r  Department shall provide organizational staff support for the Advisory \r  Board.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. The Advisory Board shall have a \r  total membership of nine members. Members \r  of the Advisory Board shall \r  be appointed as follows: the Director of the \r  Department of Environmental Quality, or the \r  Director\'s designee; the State Health Commissioner, or the Commissioner\'s designee; \r  the Secretary of Commerce and Trade, or the \r  Secretary\'s designee; and six members \r  appointed by the Governor to include one representative \r  of mattress retailers; one representative of the mattress manufacturing \r  industry; one mattress recycler; one representative of an \r  environmental advocacy group; one representative of the Virginia \r  Association of Counties; and one consumer representative. Members of the Advisory \r  Board shall be \r  citizens of the Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. Advisory \r  Board members shall serve a term of four years with a maximum of two \r  consecutive terms. The remainder of any term to which a member is \r  appointed to fill a vacancy shall not constitute a term in determining the \r  member\'s eligibility for reappointment. Vacancies shall be filled in \r  the same manner as the original appointments. All members may be reappointed.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>D. The \r  Advisory Board shall have the following duties and responsibilities:</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>1. Review and evaluate the \r  structure, financing, and other aspects of the mattress stewardship program;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>2. Review and evaluate each \r  mattress stewardship program plan \r  submitted pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a> before such plan is \r  submitted to the Department for approval;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>3. Consult with and advise \r  producers or representative organizations \r  as they prepare the mattress stewardship program plan;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>4. Receive and review the \r  mattress stewardship program plans \r  submitted pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a> and make recommendations to the \r  Department regarding mattress stewardship program plan \r  approval;</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>5. Receive and \r  review annual reports submitted pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.43\">10.1-1425.43</a> and make \r  recommendations to the Department regarding the need for any plan amendments or \r  other requirements based on the Advisory Board\'s review of such reports; \r  and</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>6. Review and comment on draft \r  regulations relevant to the implementation of the mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>E. The Advisory \r  Board shall (i) meet at least quarterly until \r  the initial mattress stewardship \r  program plans have been approved, and semiannually \r  thereafter, and (ii) report \r  annually by December 1 any findings and \r  recommendations to the General Assembly and the Governor. The \r  meetings of the Advisory Board shall be held at the call of the chairman or \r  whenever the majority of the members so request.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>F. The \r  Advisory Board shall elect a chair and vice-chair from among its membership by a simple \r  majority vote. A majority of the members shall constitute a quorum.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>G. The \r  Advisory Board may adopt bylaws for the operation of the Advisory \r  Board\'s business in accordance with this article.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.45\">10.1-1425.45</a>. Powers and \r  duties of the Department.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. The Department may \r  cooperate with departments, agencies, and other equivalent bodies in other \r  states in order to further the objectives of this article.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. The Department shall require producers or \r  representative organizations to translate all \r  educational materials into the languages spoken by local populations and to \r  ensure that collection infrastructure is accessible to residents with \r  disabilities.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>C. The Department shall make, adopt, \r  promulgate, and enforce such regulations \r  as may be necessary to carry out the provisions of this article.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.46\">10.1-1425.46</a>. Civil penalties for \r  violations of the mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>A. Any retailer that violates \r  the provisions of this article is subject to a civil penalty not to exceed $100 \r  per day for each day of violation.</ins></p>\r  \r   <p><ins>B. A producer \r  or representative organization that violates the provisions of this article is \r  subject to a civil penalty not to exceed $1,000 per day for each day of violation. A \r  civil penalty imposed on a representative organization under this subsection \r  creates a joint and several obligation of the representative \r  organization and each producer registered \r  with the representative organization. No \r  representative organization shall use any money collected through a mattress \r  stewardship assessment to pay a civil penalty \r  imposed under this subsection. Such penalty shall be \r  collected by the attorney for the Commonwealth for the locality where the \r  violation occurred, and the proceeds shall be paid into the state treasury and \r  deposited by the State Treasurer into the Litter Control and Recycling Fund \r  established pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1422.01\">10.1-1422.01</a>.</ins></p>',NULL,0,'2024-01-10 14:55:05','2025-11-06 00:15:32'),
(1046598,73984,'HB745H1',30,'2024-02-07','<br><center><b>HOUSE BILL NO. 745</b></center>\r \r <center>AMENDMENT IN THE NATURE OF A SUBSTITUTE</center>\r \r <center>(Proposed by the House Committee on Agriculture, Chesapeake and\r Natural Resources</center>\r \r <center>on February 7, 2024)</center>\r \r <center>(Patron Prior to Substitute--Delegate Laufer)</center>\r \r <i>A BILL to amend the Code of Virginia by adding in Chapter 14\r of Title 10.1 an article numbered 3.8, consisting of sections numbered\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a> through <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.45\">10.1-1425.45</a>, relating to mattress stewardship program\r established; civil penalty.</i>\r \r <p>Be it enacted by the General Assembly of Virginia:</p>\r \r <p>1. That the Code of Virginia is amended by adding in Chapter\r 14 of Title 10.1 an article numbered 3.8, consisting of sections numbered\r <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a> through <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.45\">10.1-1425.45</a>, as follows:</p>\r \r <center><ins>Article 3.8.</ins></center>\r \r <center><ins>Mattress Stewardship Program.</ins></center>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.40\">10.1-1425.40</a>. Definitions.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>As used in this article, unless the context requires a\r different meaning:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Consumer&quot; means an owner or a purchaser of a\r mattress, including a person, business, corporation, limited partnership,\r nonprofit organization, or governmental entity in the Commonwealth, and\r including the ultimate purchaser, owner, or lessee of a mattress.\r &quot;Consumer&quot; does not include a government organization or other party\r that obtains one or more used mattresses in the course of collecting used\r mattresses for recycling for the purposes of this article or through the\r ordinary collection and handling of municipal solid waste.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Discarded mattress&quot; means any mattress that a\r consumer discarded, intends to discard, or is abandoned in the Commonwealth and\r that is unacceptable for recycling because it is contaminated, wet, crushed, or\r would otherwise pose a health or safety risk to personnel or equipment.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Foundation&quot; means a ticking-covered structure\r used to support a mattress or sleep surface. The structure may include\r constructed frames, foam, box springs, or other materials, used alone or in\r combination with other products.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress&quot; means a resilient material or\r combination of materials that is enclosed by a ticking, is used alone or in\r combination with other products, and is intended for or promoted for sleeping\r on. &quot;Mattress&quot; includes a foundation. &quot;Mattress&quot; does not\r include:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. An unattached mattress pad or topper that is designed to\r be used on top of or in addition to a mattress;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. A waterbed, air mattress, or any other product that\r contains liquid-filled or gas-filled ticking and that does not contain\r upholstery material between the ticking and the mattress core;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. A carriage, basket, dressing table, stroller, playpen,\r infant carrier, lounge pad, crib bumper, crib mattress, bassinet mattress, or\r any other product manufactured for young children; or</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>4. A sleeping bag, pillow, futon, sleeper sofa, fold-out\r sofa bed, mattress foundation, car bed, or any furniture that otherwise does\r not contain a detachable mattress.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress core&quot; means the principal support\r system that is present in a mattress, including springs, foam, an air bladder,\r a water bladder, or resilient filling.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress recycling fee&quot; means the uniform per\r unit amount added to the price of a new or renovated mattress at the point of\r sale to a consumer, collected by a retailer, and remitted to the mattress\r recycling organization to fund the mattress stewardship program by distributing\r the organization\'s costs uniformly over all mattresses sold in the\r Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress recycling organization&quot; or\r &quot;organization&quot; means a nonprofit organization formed by a qualified\r industry association to implement a mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress recycling plan&quot; or &quot;plan&quot;\r means the plan for recycling discarded mattresses developed by the mattress\r recycling organization and approved by the Department.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Mattress stewardship program&quot; or\r &quot;program&quot; means the program to recycle mattresses discarded in the\r Commonwealth established in § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.41\">10.1-1425.41</a>.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Producer&quot; means (i) a manufacturer of a mattress\r that sells, offers for sale, or distributes mattresses to consumers in the\r Commonwealth under the producer\'s own name or brand or (ii) an individual that\r imports mattresses into the United States to sell, offer for sale, or\r distribute in the Commonwealth. &quot;Producer&quot; includes the owner of a\r mattress trademark or brand.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Qualified industry association&quot; means the\r International Sleep Products Association or a successor of that organization,\r or a group of mattress producers that collectively represent at least 35\r percent of the volume of mattresses manufactured in the United States.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Recycle&quot; or &quot;recycling&quot; means the\r process of collecting, sorting, cleansing, treating, and reconstituting\r materials that would otherwise become solid waste and returning them to the\r economic mainstream in the form of raw material for new, reused, or\r reconstituted products that meet the quality standards necessary to be used in\r the marketplace.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Recycler&quot; means a person that engages in the\r manual or mechanical separation of mattresses to substantially recover\r components and commodities contained in mattresses for the purpose of\r recycling.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Renovate&quot; means to alter a discarded mattress\r for resale in compliance with any regulations promulgated pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/32.1-12\">32.1-12</a>\r by the Board of Health through replacing the ticking or filling, adding\r additional filling, or replacing components of the discarded mattress with new\r or recycled materials. &quot;Renovate&quot; does not include (i) stripping a\r discarded mattress of the ticking or filling without adding new material or\r (ii) the sanitization or sterilization of a discarded mattress without other\r alteration to the discarded mattress.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Renovator&quot; means a person who renovates used\r mattresses for the purpose of sale, or offering for sale, in the Commonwealth\r in compliance with any regulations promulgated pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/32.1-12\">32.1-12</a> by the\r Board of Health.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Retailer&quot; means any person that sells or offers\r for sale mattresses to consumers in the Commonwealth through any means,\r including remote offering, sales outlets or catalogs, electronically through\r the Internet, by telephone, or through the mail. Such means also include sales\r or offers for sale to lodging establishments, educational facilities, health\r facilities, and similar types of customers.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Sale&quot; or &quot;sell&quot; means any transfer of\r title, including remote sales conducted through sales outlets, catalogues, or\r the Internet or any other similar electronic means.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Sanitization&quot; means the process of treating\r renovated mattresses in accordance with processes approved by the State Health\r Commissioner.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Sterilization&quot; means the mitigation of any\r deleterious substances or organisms, including pathogens that cause human\r disease, fungi, and insects, from a mattress or filling using a chemical or\r heat process.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>&quot;Ticking&quot; means the outermost layer of fabric or\r related material of a mattress. &quot;Ticking&quot; does not include another\r layer of fabric or related material that is quilted together with, or otherwise\r attached to, the outermost layer of fabric or related material.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.41\">10.1-1425.41</a>. Mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A. To the extent feasible, cost-effective, and\r environmentally efficient, the mattress recycling program will increase the\r recycling of commercially useful materials in discarded mattresses and\r substantially reduce public agency costs for the end-of-life management of\r discarded mattresses by requiring mattress producers that sell or offer\r mattresses for sale to consumers in the Commonwealth to develop, finance, and\r implement a program in accordance with this article to collect and recycle\r mattresses discarded in the Commonwealth using existing solid waste collection\r systems and other mattress collection methods.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. On or before January 1, 2026, a qualified industry\r association may establish an organization to develop, implement, and administer\r the mattress stewardship program in the Commonwealth established pursuant to\r this article and request that the Department certify such organization as a\r mattress recycling organization. Within 30 days of receipt of a request for\r certification, the Department shall notify the requesting qualified industry\r association of its decision whether or not to certify such organization as a\r mattress recycling organization.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>C. On or before June 1, 2026, each producer, retailer, and\r renovator shall register with the mattress recycling organization. Each\r producer shall identify the mattress brand or brands that it sells or offers\r for sale to consumers in the Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>D. On or before July 1, 2026, the certified mattress\r recycling organization shall submit to the Department for approval a plan for\r the establishment of a mattress stewardship program. Such plan shall:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. Identify the producers, brands, retailers, and\r renovators registered with the organization;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. Describe how the organization will collect, transport,\r and recycle discarded mattresses, including contracting with third parties to\r provide some or all of those services, and the approximate time following\r implementation of the program required to implement various aspects of the\r approved program;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. Propose a uniform per unit mattress recycling fee that\r is sufficient to cover all costs of establishing and administering the program,\r including a financial reserve sufficient to operate the program over a\r multiyear period of time in a fiscally prudent and responsible manner, and\r describe how the retailer will collect such fee from the consumer and remit\r such fees to the organization;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>4. Describe sound management practices that the\r organization will communicate to third parties under contract with the\r organization that the third parties may use to protect worker health and\r safety;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>5. Describe the criteria entities under contract with the\r organization will apply in determining whether a mattress should be rejected as\r unacceptable for recycling because it is contaminated, wet, crushed, or would\r otherwise pose a health or safety risk to personnel or equipment, and how the\r solid waste sector would like to dispose of such mattresses;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>6. Describe how the stewardship organization will (i) work\r with existing recyclers and mattress collection infrastructure; (ii) consult\r with state and local governments and other stakeholders; and (iii) conduct\r research as necessary to improve the collection and recycling of mattresses,\r including research to identify new markets and uses for recycled material;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>7. Describe how the organization will solicit, recruit, and\r compensate mattress waste management entities including (i) existing municipal,\r private, and nonprofit entities in the Commonwealth that transport, handle, or\r recycle discarded mattresses; (ii) minority-owned, woman-owned, and\r veteran-owned business enterprises; and (iii) businesses that prioritize the\r training and hiring of individuals who are reentering society after\r imprisonment, experiencing homelessness and housing insecurity, in addiction\r recovery, disabled, or are otherwise socioeconomically or historically\r marginalized or disadvantaged;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>8. Propose an operating budget for the partial calendar\r year between the date on which the program is launched and the end of such\r calendar year and the following full calendar year. Such budgets shall provide\r the basis for the proposed per unit mattress recycling fee required by\r subdivision 3. Such budgets shall include (i) preoperational expenses that the\r mattress recycling organization incurred in developing the proposed plan, costs\r of organizing the collection and recycling networks, and costs of related\r actions necessary to prepare the program for operation and (ii) a financial\r reserve sufficient to operate the program over a multiyear period of time in a\r fiscally prudent and responsible manner;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>9. Describe how the organization will educate consumers,\r producers, and retailers to promote the recycling of discarded mattresses and\r options available to consumers for recycling discarded mattresses;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>10. Provide a plan in case of closure of the program;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>11. Describe how the program will minimize public sector\r involvement in, and financial responsibility for, the management of discarded\r mattresses by (i) promoting mattress recycling and (ii) negotiating agreements\r to collect, transport, and recycle mattresses and their components;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>12. Describe how the program will provide for convenient\r mattress collection in the Commonwealth by (i) providing free access to drop\r off locations for mattresses from residential, commercial, institutional, or\r other sources; (ii) providing free collection of discarded mattresses from any\r person, municipal transfer station, or other solid waste facility in the Commonwealth\r that has accumulated and segregated a minimum of 100 mattresses discarded in\r the Commonwealth for collection at one time; and (iii) perform geographic\r modeling analysis to determine the efficient number and placement of sites for\r collecting discarded mattresses such that, by the end of the second year of the\r program\'s operation, at least one collection site will be located within a\r 25-mile radius of 75 percent of the Commonwealth\'s residents;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>13. Include performance goals for (i) based on data that\r the Department collects annually from solid waste facilities in the\r Commonwealth and other sources, the percentage of discarded mattresses that are\r collected by recyclers participating in the program; (ii) the percentage of the\r weight of discarded mattresses processed by recyclers participating in the\r program that is recycled; (iii) based on data that the Department collects from\r renovators operating in the Commonwealth, the percentage of discarded\r mattresses that are renovated; and</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>14. Include other information requested by the Department\r that is reasonably related to compliance with the recycling plan and the\r requirements of this article and that the organization can reasonably provide.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>E. An independent certified public accountant shall\r evaluate at the organization\'s expense the mattress recycling fee proposed in\r the plan, and any subsequent changes to such fee that the organization\r proposes, to confirm whether such fee covers the cost of establishing and\r administering the program but does not exceed such costs, including a financial\r reserve sufficient to operate the program over a multiyear period of time in a\r fiscally prudent and responsible manner.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>F. The Department shall review the plan proposed by the\r organization and the fee evaluation report prepared by the independent\r financial auditor pursuant to subsection E.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>G. The Department shall (i) approve the plan proposed by\r the organization if it determines that the plan, including the proposed budget\r and proposed mattress recycling fee, complies with the requirements of\r subsection E and (ii) list on its website the producers, brands, retailers, and\r renovators that are registered with the organization.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>H. The mattress recycling organization that submits a\r proposed plan for approval shall pay to the Department (i) a plan review fee\r that reimburses the Department\'s actual cost of plan review and (ii) an annual\r fee that reimburses the Department for its actual costs associated with program\r compliance oversight, including the Department\'s review of the organization\'s\r annual report, but excluding costs incurred by the Department in connection\r with the Mattress Stewardship Program Advisory Board.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>I. The Department shall reevaluate the plan approved\r pursuant to subsection G five years following approval, and may reevaluate the\r plan sooner if the Department concludes that the organization has not met its\r targets or a change in circumstances warrants reevaluation. If necessary, the\r organization may propose changes to the plan for the Department\'s approval.\r Thereafter, the Department shall reevaluate the plan every five years.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>J. Following the Department\'s approval of the plan,\r including the budget and mattress recycling fee:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. The organization shall implement its mattress\r stewardship plan within one year after approval of the plan.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. At least 90 days before the date on which program\r implementation will commence, the organization shall notify all producers,\r retailers, and renovators of that implementation date and their obligations\r under the plan.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. Upon plan implementation:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>a. No producer or retailer shall sell or offer for sale a\r brand of mattress to any person in the Commonwealth unless both the producer of\r the brand and the retailer are registered with the organization. A retailer\r shall be considered compliant with this requirement if (i) it is registered\r with the organization and (ii) on the date the retailer orders a mattress from\r a producer, the producer is listed on the Department\'s website as having\r registered with the organization.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>b. Each producer, retailer, or renovator that sells a\r mattress to a consumer in the Commonwealth shall add the mattress recycling fee\r to the purchase price of the mattress. Such fee shall be clearly visible as a\r separate line item on the invoice, receipt, or functionally equivalent billing\r document that the seller provides to the consumer. The seller shall remit such\r fee collected to the mattress recycling organization. The mattress recycling\r organization shall determine the rules and procedures that are necessary and\r proper to implement the collection of the fee in a fair, efficient, and lawful\r manner.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>c. The mattress recycling organization shall provide\r producers, retailers, renovators, and consumers with educational materials\r regarding the program that include (i) information regarding available\r end-of-life management options offered through such program for discarded\r mattresses and (ii) information that notifies consumers that a fee to cover the\r costs of implementing such program is added to the purchase price of all\r mattresses sold to consumers in the Commonwealth. A retailer shall provide such\r educational materials to consumers at the point of sale.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>K. 1. In the first 12 months during which the mattress\r recycling fee is collected, the mattress recycling organization may change the\r amount of the mattress recycling fee and shall provide no less than 90 days\'\r notice to the public before any change in the amount of such fee takes place.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. After one year from the date when the collection of the\r mattress recycling fee commences, the mattress recycling organization may\r change the amount of such fee, but the organization shall not change the amount\r of such fee more frequently than annually unless the organization provides good\r cause to change the fee earlier and shall provide no less than 180 days\' notice\r to the public before the change in the amount of such fee takes place.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. The mattress recycling organization may conduct an audit\r of those parties required to remit the fee to the mattress recycling\r organization to verify that the fees paid are proper and accurate and to\r confirm that all parties required by this article to pay or collect the fee are\r paying or collecting the proper amount. The audit shall be carried out in accordance\r with generally accepted auditing practices and shall be limited in scope to\r confirm whether the fee has been properly collected on all sales of mattresses\r to consumers in the Commonwealth. The mattress recycling organization shall\r hire independent third-party auditors to conduct the audits. The organization\r shall provide to the Department a copy of such audit reports.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>L. No mattress collection site that participates in a\r mattress stewardship program shall charge any additional fee for accepting a discarded\r mattress from consumers for recycling, unless the site itself must dispose of a\r mattress that is unacceptable for recycling because it is contaminated, wet,\r crushed, or would otherwise pose a health or safety risk to personnel or\r equipment.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>M. An action taken by a mattress recycling organization or\r entities registered with the organization that relates to any of the following\r is not a violation of the Commonwealth\'s antitrust, restraint of trade, or\r unfair competition laws:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. The creation, implementation, or management of a\r mattress recycling plan approved by the Department and the types or quantities\r of used mattresses recycled or otherwise managed pursuant to such plan;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. The cost and structure of an approved plan; or</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. The establishment, administration, collection, or\r disbursement of the mattress recycling fees associated with funding the\r implementation of the mattress recycling organization and related functions the\r organization performs.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>This subsection shall not authorize any person to engage in\r activities or to conspire to engage in activities that constitute per se\r violations of state or federal antitrust laws that are not otherwise authorized\r by this article.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a>. Annual reporting requirements.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A. Beginning June 1 of the year following the calendar year\r during which the mattress recycling organization implemented an approved plan,\r and on June 1 annually thereafter, the mattress recycling organization shall\r submit to the Department and publish online a report that details its mattress\r stewardship program during the preceding calendar year, including the\r following:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. A description of the methods used to collect, transport,\r and process mattresses discarded in the Commonwealth;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. The quantity and type of discarded mattresses collected\r in the Commonwealth by (i) collection site or collection method and (ii) method\r of disposition, including reuse, recycling, and other methods of processing, or\r method of disposal if mattresses were found unacceptable for recycling or for\r other reasons;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. The quantity of each category of materials recycled and\r the quantity of otherwise disposed of materials;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>4. The uses for the recycled materials;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>5. The number of illegally dumped mattresses, based on data\r the Department collects from localities in the Commonwealth;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>6. The audit report of the mattress recycling\r organization\'s accounting books conducted at the organization\'s expense by an\r independent certified public accountant retained by the organization;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>7. Samples of educational materials used to inform\r consumers about mattress recycling, a summary of public education efforts, and\r an evaluation of the effectiveness of such educational materials and public\r education efforts;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>8. An evaluation of why unrecycled mattresses were not\r recycled;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>9. The total number of mattresses sold to consumers in the\r Commonwealth in the previous calendar year, based on data that the mattress\r recycling organization collects in connection with the mattress recycling fee\r remittance process;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>10. Strategies to address discarded mattresses that were\r not included in such program and that were illegally dumped;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>11. Reports on research activities and pilot programs\r conducted or funded by the mattress recycling organization;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>12. An analysis of how the data required by this subsection\r has changed over time;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>13. An evaluation of the effectiveness of methods and\r processes used to achieve the goals of such program, including information on\r progress made toward achieving such goals, an explanation of why any goals were\r not met during the previous calendar year, and information on any efforts that\r will be taken to improve progress toward meeting such goals in the future, if\r applicable;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>14. Recommendations for any changes to such program,\r including information relevant to compliance with the plan; and</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>15. Other information requested by the Department that is\r reasonably related to compliance with the recycling plan and the requirements\r of this article and that the organization can reasonably provide.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. The Department shall review the annual report and (i)\r evaluate the total revenues and expenses of the mattress stewardship program to\r determine whether the revenue earned from the mattress recycling fee meets or\r exceeds the costs of the program, including a financial reserve sufficient to\r operate the program over a multiyear period of time in a fiscally prudent and\r responsible manner, and (ii) determine whether the program is being implemented\r in accordance with the organization\'s approved plan. If the Department\r determines that the annual report complies with the requirements of subsection\r A and that the revenue earned from the mattress recycling fee does not exceed\r the costs of the program, the Department shall approve the annual report. The\r mattress recycling organization shall post the approved annual report on the\r organization\'s website. If the Department determines that the annual report\r does not comply with the requirements of subsection A or that the revenue\r earned from the mattress recycling fee exceeds the costs of the program, the\r mattress recycling organization shall submit to the Department a plan on how\r the organization will address the noncompliance in the upcoming year.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>C. Financial, production, or sales data reported to the\r Department by a producer, retailer, mattress recycling organization, or\r recycler shall be kept confidential by the Department and shall not be subject\r to public inspection. The Department may release summary data that does not\r disclose financial, production, or sales data of a producer, retailer, or\r mattress recycling organization.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.43\">10.1-1425.43</a>. Mattress Stewardship Program Advisory Board\r established; membership; duties and responsibilities; meetings; annual report.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A. There is hereby established the Mattress Stewardship\r Program Advisory Board, referred to in this section as &quot;the Advisory\r Board,&quot; for the purpose of advising the Department on the proper\r implementation and sustainability of the mattress stewardship program. The\r Department shall provide organizational staff support for the Advisory Board.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. The Advisory Board shall have a total membership of nine\r members. Members of the Advisory Board shall be appointed as follows: the\r Director of the Department of Environmental Quality, or the Director\'s\r designee; the State Health Commissioner, or the Commissioner\'s designee; the\r Secretary of Commerce and Trade, or the Secretary\'s designee; and six members\r appointed by the Governor to include one representative of mattress retailers;\r one representative of the mattress manufacturing industry; one mattress\r recycler; one representative of an environmental advocacy group; one\r representative of the Virginia Association of Counties; and one consumer\r representative. Members of the Advisory Board shall be citizens of the\r Commonwealth.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>C. Advisory Board members shall serve a term of four years\r with a maximum of two consecutive terms. The remainder of any term to which a\r member is appointed to fill a vacancy shall not constitute a term in\r determining the member\'s eligibility for reappointment. Vacancies shall be filled\r in the same manner as the original appointments. All members may be\r reappointed.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>D. The Advisory Board shall have the following duties and\r responsibilities:</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>1. Review and evaluate the structure, financing, and other\r aspects of the mattress stewardship program;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>2. Consult with and advise producers or representative\r organizations as they prepare the mattress stewardship program plan;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>3. Receive and review the mattress stewardship program\r plans submitted pursuant to § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.41\">10.1-1425.41</a> and make recommendations to the\r Department regarding mattress stewardship program plan approval;</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>4. Receive and review annual reports submitted pursuant to\r § <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.42\">10.1-1425.42</a> and make recommendations to the Department regarding the need\r for any plan amendments or other requirements based on the Advisory Board\'s\r review of such reports; and</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>5. Review and comment on draft regulations relevant to the\r implementation of the mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>E. The Advisory Board shall (i) meet at least quarterly\r until the initial mattress stewardship program plans have been approved, and\r semiannually thereafter, and (ii) report annually by December 1 any findings\r and recommendations to the General Assembly and the Governor. The meetings of\r the Advisory Board shall be held at the call of the chairman or whenever the\r majority of the members so request.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>F. The Advisory Board shall elect a chair and vice-chair\r from among its membership by a simple majority vote. A majority of the members\r shall constitute a quorum.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>G. The Advisory Board may adopt bylaws for the operation of\r the Advisory Board\'s business in accordance with this article.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.44\">10.1-1425.44</a>. Powers and duties of the Department.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A. The Department may cooperate with departments, agencies,\r and other equivalent bodies in other states in order to further the objectives\r of this article.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. The Department shall require the mattress recycling\r organization to translate all educational materials into the languages spoken\r by local populations.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>C. The Department shall require solid waste facilities,\r renovators, localities, and other relevant entities to report to the Department\r any information that the mattress recycling organization will need to prepare\r its annual report and will provide aggregated data to the organization for this\r purpose.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>D. The Department shall make, adopt, promulgate, and\r enforce such regulations as may be necessary to carry out the provisions of\r this article.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>§ <a href=\"http://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/10.1-1425.45\">10.1-1425.45</a>. Civil penalties for violations of the\r mattress stewardship program.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>A. Any retailer that violates the provisions of this\r article is subject to a civil penalty not to exceed $100 per day for each day\r of violation.</ins></p>\r \r <p><ins>B. A producer or mattress recycling organization that\r violates the provisions of this article is subject to a civil penalty not to\r exceed $1,000 per day for each day of violation. In evaluating whether to\r impose a civil penalty, and in determining the amount of such civil penalty,\r the Department shall take into account the materiality of the violation, whether\r the violation is wholly or partially the result of factors beyond the control\r of the producer or mattress recycling organization, whether the producer or\r mattress recycling organization has made a good faith effort to comply with the\r provisions of this article, and whether the violation can be addressed through\r means other than a civil penalty.</ins></p>',NULL,0,'2024-02-07 21:55:01','2025-11-06 00:15:32');
