INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES
(16055,90278,73960,'Bryan Miller','example@example.com',NULL,'127.37.199.184','This HB 721 is a bad bill. It is not a bill against rent-gouging, but a bill against raising rent to merely keep up with inflation. That is, a very severe rent control bill. It would authorize local governments to adopt ordinances that limit rent increases, and once adopted, they could only limit increases to less than or equal to inflation (indeed, it would limit rent increases to less than inflation, when the percentage of the Consumer Price Index exceeds a specified level). The ordinance could not even allow larger increases to keep up with inflation or pay for renovations that are needed!\n\nMost economists think rent control is a bad idea: In a 1992 poll, 93 percent of them agreed that rent control reduces the quantity and quality of housing available. As The Wall Street Journal said, \"If there’s any consensus in economics, it’s that rent control achieves the opposite of its intended goal. It leads to housing shortages by discouraging new development and maintenance of existing properties.\"\n\nRent control also reduces housing values -- cutting the tax revenue needed to fund schools -- and cuts new housing construction -- as a study of St. Paul, Minnesota, found. Landlords respond to rent control by cutting renovations and sometimes maintenance.','comment','published','n','2024-01-17 20:00:08','2024-01-17 20:00:08'),
(16146,90412,73960,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.193.36.220','Rent control has failed everywhere and always.','comment','published','n','2024-01-28 15:41:27','2024-01-28 15:41:27');
