INSERT INTO `fiscal_impact_statements` VALUES
(40594,72902,'F122',NULL,'<p>This legislation, HB33, would have a fiscal impact on the Virginia Department of Health (VDH). It would require the agency to study the occurrence of microplastics in the Commonwealth\'s public drinking water and develop recommendations for reducing microplastics. \n\nThe expenditure impact for this legislation is estimated as follows:\n\n- Fiscal Year 2025: $4,404,000 from the General Fund\n- Fiscal Year 2026 to 2030: No additional expenditure impact\n\nThe estimated cost is based on the assumption that all 2,860 waterworks in Virginia would need to be analyzed to determine current levels of microplastics in drinking water. California\'s standardized methods for sampling and testing microplastics cost an estimated $1,000 to $2,000 per sample. Assuming an average cost of $1,500 per sample, analyzing samples from all waterworks would cost $4,290,000.\n\nIf the legislation intends to sample a subset of waterworks and project a statewide analysis, a minimum of 1,000 samples would be necessary at an estimated cost of $1,500,000.\n\nIn addition to the study, the legislation also requires the establishment of a work group to evaluate existing approaches to reducing microplastics in drinking water. VDH does not have the resources to provide the necessary administrative and technical support for the work group and would need to hire a qualified contractor. Based on a similar legislation in the 2023 session, VDH estimated a contractor would charge $96,000 for administrative and technical support, including the cost to complete a report, and an additional $18,000 for a comprehensive review of existing approaches.\n\nIn summary, the fiscal impact of this legislation on the Virginia Department of Health is estimated to be $4,404,000 in the fiscal year 2025.</p>\n        <p class=\"openai\">Summary generated automatically by OpenAI.</p>','2025-01-12 03:10:40','2025-01-12 03:10:40');
